tools/pipeline/ledger.json:3391:     "ev": "Agent seat (fable5) adjudication 2026-08-29; census, osiop:500-505, asc:1395-1400 and the raw-vs-call grep contamination all re-verified by the main loop against source",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:3394:     "note": "VERDICT: **`return 0` IS REFUTED -- not merely a sketch, but a MEASURED-BY-READING\nBEHAVIOURAL REGRESSION on two controllers with ZERO benefit on the third.  The right shape\nis `diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp, d, 0x05, 0x20, 0x00)` PLUS the fall-through\n`return 1` -- the file's own refusal idiom at four shipped sites.**  Every claim below was\nre-verified by me against the source before recording.\n\n*** MY CENSUS WAS CONTAMINATED AND SO IS THE ROW'S.  Both counted the COMMENT HEADER and the\nDEFINITION as calls. ***\n\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message'          -> 25   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message(xferp'    -> 23   CALLS\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition'                     ->  5   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp'               ->  3   CALLS\n    grep -c $'^\\tcase '                                             -> 22   top-level labels\n\nSo **the row's \"the only one of 25\" is wrong under ANY unit** -- 25 is the contaminated raw\ncount.  The correct statement is **\"the only one of 20 TOP-LEVEL ARMS\"** (22 labels forming\n19 bodies, since MODE_SENSE/10, READ/READ_10 and WRITE/WRITE_10 share bodies, plus the\ndefault).  My own `~30` counted NESTED labels.  *A re-deriver caution worth keeping: \n`grep -E '^\\tcase'` silently matches NOTHING, because `\\t` is a literal `t` in ERE -- use\n`$'\\t'`.  I hit that exact trap on this file earlier today.*\n\n**THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM SURVIVES, verified arm by arm**: all 19 bodies call the status helper\non every completing path.  ONE QUALIFIER: at PATH level the two `return 2` first passes also\nset no status -- correctly, because the command is incomplete and the DATA_OUT re-entry sets\nit, and all three SCSI controllers re-call.  My return enumeration was exact.\n\n*** WHY `return 0` IS WORSE THAN A SKETCH -- three consumers, each read: ***\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:500-505`** -- `res == 0` sets **`d->scripts_running = 0`**.  A guest\n    probing one unsupported opcode **kills its own SCRIPTS engine**; no status phase ever.\n  * **`dev_asc.c:1395-1400`** -- `ok == 0` gives `STATE_DISCONNECTED` + `NCRINTR_DIS`.  The\n    guest sees a target DISCONNECT instead of CHECK CONDITION: retry/reset instead of\n    REQUEST SENSE.  **This also refutes the row's own state_note, which says asc does not\n    test for 0 at all** -- its select path does.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c:1528-1541`** -- the `res == 0` arm sets **no error bit**, and `status_byte()`\n    raises `WDCS_ERR` only from `d->error`.  So on wdc, `return 0` is guest-INDISTINGUISHABLE\n    from success-with-no-data.\n\n> **`return 0` helps no consumer and harms two.**  That is a stronger refutation than my\n> structural one, and it is the form a future round should cite.\n\n**THE FIX, WITH ITS WARRANT:** CHECK-CONDITION-then-`return 1` is ALREADY this file's refusal\nidiom at four shipped sites, and all four controllers demonstrably handle it today.  `d` is\nnon-NULL in the default arm (the NULL path returned earlier).  #417's latch semantics come\nfree: REQUEST SENSE reads `d->sense_key/asc/ascq` and the next success retires them.\n*The state_note's cited precedent is the WRONG one -- that shape exists only because `d` is\nNULL there and `check_condition` would dereference it; its own comments say `// ?` and `TODO`.*\n\n**MUST NOT BREAK:** (a) do NOT allocate `data_in` -- all three SCSI controllers pick the\nDATA_IN phase off `data_in != NULL`, and status-only completion is already TEST_UNIT_READY's\nshape; (b) leave the pre-switch not-connected `return 0` alone; (c) `est/` byte-identical\npropagation, shared file; (d) **the fix ALSO cures `mb89352halt`'s mechanism** -- `status_len`\nbecomes 1, so the PH_STAT read no longer trips 'read longer than buffer?' into\n`cpu->running = 0`, consistent with the standing ONE-ROUND-for-both ruling.\n\n*** THE ATAPI ROUTE IS REAL AND IS THE **WRONG WITNESS CHANNEL**, which inverts what I\nassumed when I raised it. ***  `dev_wdc.c:1515` does feed packet commands in, and `res == 1`\nwith no data gives PHASE_COMPLETED and no error bit.  **But `atapi_st->status` is read\nNOWHERE in dev_wdc.c** -- so an ATAPI witness stays RED after a dispatcher-only fix, because\nit witnesses the COMBINED dispatcher+wdc defect.  The discriminating channels for THIS fix\nare **asc** (ICCS fifo byte 0x00 -> 0x02, pmax rig) or **mb89352** (loudest pre-fix symptom\non record: the emulated CPU stops).  The wdc consumer repair is its own hunk in a different\nfile and should be filed separately -- the one-omission-in-one-shared-function warrant does\nnot stretch to it.\n\nRANKING: raises reachability BREADTH within the band, not harm class.  Keep rank 3.  Plausible\nbut UNMEASURED: MMC opcodes 0x46 (GET CONFIGURATION) and 0x4A (GET EVENT STATUS NOTIFICATION)\nare not among the 19 arms and period ATAPI attach code issues them routinely -- one flagged\nboot would settle it.\n\n**FILED, NOT FIXED (a sibling this round does not touch):** `SPACE`'s NESTED `default:`\n`fatal()`s and then FALLS THROUGH to the status helper, **telling the guest GOOD for an\nunimplemented SPACE type**.  Same false-success class, inside a KNOWN arm rather than the\ndefault one, so b120u's fix does not reach it.  b120r-shaped."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:3399:   "state_note": "*** L4 WRONG RECORD, CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-27. An adversarial opus pass REFUTED THE MECHANISM THIS ROW STATED BELOW. The defect is real; my explanation of it was false, and the true picture is four different wrong outcomes rather than one. ***\n\n**WHAT I GOT WRONG.** I wrote that `scsi_transfer_alloc()`'s `memset(p, 0, ...)` leaves a status BYTE of 0x00, which the guest reads as GOOD. **`status` is `unsigned char *` (`diskimage.h:196-197`)** -- so the memset leaves `status == NULL` and `status_len == 0`. **There is no zeroed status byte anywhere.** Nothing reads a 0x00 out of that struct. The conclusion happened to hold for one controller, for an entirely different reason.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the structural half:** the `default:` arm is the **only one of 25** that never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()` or `diskimage__return_check_condition()` -- verified by grepping every case/helper pair -- and it falls through to `return 1`, which the function's own header (`:263-266`) documents as the success token (`2` = expects DATA_OUT, `1` = otherwise ok, `0` = error).\n\n*** THE HONEST STATEMENT: ONE OMISSION, FOUR DIFFERENT WRONG OUTCOMES. ***\n  * **`dev_asc.c` -- guest IS told GOOD**, but via an explicit `else fifo_write(0x00)` fallback at `:1284-1292`, which `git log -S` places in the unmodified upstream 0.7.0 import, NOT a fork change. Right conclusion, wrong reason.\n  * **`dev_mb89352.c` -- REFUTES the 'told success' claim outright.** `data_in == NULL` sets `phase = PH_STAT` (`:284`); the guest's PH_STAT read takes `p = status (NULL), len = 0`, trips the `read longer than buffer?` arm (`:173`) and **sets `cpu->running = 0`**. The guest is not told anything -- the emulated CPU is STOPPED. **Filed separately as [[mb89352halt]].**\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:573-582`** -- `while (... && i < status_len)` with `status_len == 0` writes **no status byte at all**; the guest's destination memory keeps its prior contents.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c` (ATAPI)** -- `atapi_st->status` is read nowhere in the file; `res == 1` with `data_in == NULL` gives `PHASE_COMPLETED` with `WDCS_ERR` unset: a successful packet command.\n\n> **That is a BETTER case than the row originally made, not a weaker one** -- a single omitted completion step producing four distinct wrong behaviours across four controllers. But it is not the claim as written, and the claim as written was the one being ranked.\n\n**A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THE ORIGINAL RECORD MISSED:** the helper also RETIRES LATCHED SENSE (the `#417` fix, `:187-188`). The `default:` arm skips that too -- so an unimplemented command following a failed one leaves `sense_key/asc/ascq` latched, and a follow-up REQUEST SENSE attributes the OLD failure to the command that just ran. That is exactly the regression `#417` was written to remove, reachable by a different door.\n\n**FIX SHAPE, now pinned by the in-tree idiom at `:319-327`:** call the helper, set `status[0] = 0x02` (CHECK CONDITION), then `return 0`. Note `return 0` is **distinguishable on only two of four controllers** -- osiop stops the SCRIPTS engine, wdc completes with a latched warning, while asc and mb89352 do not test for 0 at all -- so the status buffer, not the return value, is what carries the refusal.\n\n--- the original record follows, kept so the correction is checkable ---\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, GUEST-REACHABLE, and the highest-severity bare-title row worked so far. Established by reading; the fix idiom ALREADY EXISTS in this file. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c:1380-1388`, the dispatcher's `default:` arm:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n        debug(\"[ UNIMPLEMENTED SCSI command 0x%02x, disk id=%i\", xferp->cmd[0], id);\n        // exit(1);\n}\ndebug(\" ]\");\nreturn 1;\n```\n\n**Three things go wrong together:**\n1. it logs through `debug()`, which is verbosity-gated and normally invisible;\n2. **it never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()`** -- every implemented arm does (`:1212`, `:1257`, `:1298`, `:1340`, `:1347`, `:1376`);\n3. it falls through to **`return 1`, the same value a SUCCESSFUL command returns.**\n\n**And the status the guest reads is GOOD, which was checked rather than assumed:** `scsi_transfer_alloc()` ends with `memset(p, 0, sizeof(struct scsi_transfer))` (`diskimage_scsicmd.c:89`), and `dev_asc.c:326-330` frees and reallocates per command. Status 0x00 IS GOOD. **So an unsupported opcode is reported to the guest as a completed, successful command.**\n\n*** THE COMMENTED-OUT `exit(1)` IS THE TELL. *** This site was de-fatalised by commenting out the kill, which removed the death without adding an error path -- the incomplete form of the de-fatalisation this fork otherwise does carefully.\n\n*** THE FIX HAS A TRANSFERRED WARRANT ALREADY IN THIS FILE, found by grepping the tree for the existing idiom rather than reasoning from scratch. *** `diskimage__return_check_condition()` is defined at `:208` and already used for exactly this class at `:823-824` -- `0x05, 0x21`, ILLEGAL REQUEST -- and again at `:909`. **The correct refusal for an unknown opcode is CHECK CONDITION with ILLEGAL REQUEST / INVALID COMMAND OPERATION CODE, and the helper to do it sits three hundred lines above the defect.**\n\n**NOT FIXED: needs a build.** The detector must show the pre-fix build reporting GOOD for an unsupported opcode and CHECK CONDITION after -- and floor clause (b) needs the `return 1` reinstated verbatim. Both are measurements. Queued behind the embargo.\n\n"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:4945:     "note": "*** MERGE WITH devexit: THEY INTERSECT AT 23 LINES AND NEITHER FILING SEAT SAW THE OTHER'S ROW. ***  Census of DEVICE_ACCESS handlers that test access LENGTH and then exit(1):\n    dev_pcc2.c 11 | dev_sgi_re.c 6 | dev_sh4.c 2 | gdrom/osiop/pvr 1 each\nA handler that cannot service a width (hlen) whose failure mode is process termination (devexit) is ONE DEFECT ON ONE LINE. Genuine merge: one fix design (the #218-#223/#438 shape), one detector shape. What survives separately is only a coverage ledger, not a round.\n\nRANKED FIRST OF THE CLUSTER on severity, not ease: exit(1) in a handler is the only outcome in the packet that destroys ALL guest state -- every process, plus whatever the disk overlay had not flushed. First round is [[sh4bcr]], witnessed at rung 3 today.\n\nThe filed witness is real and independently confirmed: ARM does emit 8-byte accesses (cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:101 `const int datalen = 8;` for LDRD/STRD, force-aligned at :229, handed to memory_rw at :233/:367), and DEV_IRQC_IRQ=0x0 / MASK=0x4 / UNMASK=0x8 means an 8-byte access at offset 0 spans two registers with OPPOSITE legal directions.\n\nA NUMBER TO CITE FROM THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE PROSE: census_exits.py on HEAD gives 397 sites (214 direct + 183 macro), 119 ACCESS, 30 device files -- not the 398/120 in circulation. The delta is #438 (cf4b083). The instrument is current by construction; the prose is not.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:5396:     "note": "*** VERDICT: THE FILE IS THE WRONG UNIT -- MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. Close as documented-only with the census as the standing answer. *** **91% IS THE KILLER:** of 118 `.c` files in src/devices/, **107 carry at least one terminating site** -- so a per-file 'stops the host' flag marks 91% of the population, because CHECK_ALLOCATION at init is near-universal (144 of 183 macro sites are INIT, and INIT is 47% of the whole tally). **A metric that flags 91% of its population ranks nothing and schedules nothing.** Only **29** files carry an ACCESS site at all, so 78 of those listed are permanently zero in the only bucket that matters. Even within ACCESS the distribution defeats per-file completeness: top 5 files are 54%, median 2, and 13 of 29 have exactly one. **THE QUEUE HAS CONFUSED THESE DENOMINATORS MORE THAN THE BRIEF ADMITTED:** it traced 114 -> 170 -> 220 -> 253/271 -> 360 across the record, with devexit's own closure note carrying two different universes in one sentence AND the warning 'RECORD THE UNIVERSE OR THE NUMBER LOOKS WRONG LATER'. The brief said 'confused once'; it is at least three times. THE THREE UNITS, and only two are needed: **FILE is a SCHEDULING unit and never a coverage denominator**; **guest-reachable ACCESS SITE (90) is the ELIGIBILITY denominator** -- with the caveat that even ACCESS contains known non-defects (dev_cons.c:80's intended exit(0), dev_ram.c:132's guest-unreachable mode), so 90 is eligibility and not a defect count; and **DE-FATALISED PREDICATE is the OBLIGATION denominator**, the only one that maps onto existing machinery (accept rows and clause (d) are both per-predicate). *** THE RECORD THE ROW ASKS FOR ALREADY EXISTS, PER-SITE, IN THE SOURCE: 51 sites across 12 files already carry a `/* #NNN: was exit(1) */` tag *** -- dev_pvr.c 25, gdrom 5, osiop 5, 8253 4, pcc2 4, and seven more. That is more precise than the per-file tally requested, written at the site, keyed by round, in the fork's own tagging ethos. Combined with `census_exits.py --by-file`, which ships and the seat ran, both halves are satisfied. BOUNDED RESIDUAL: nothing answers 'of the 90 ACCESS sites, which already carry a tag?' -- a one-flag addition (`--tagged`), discharged once, not a standing policy. CONSTRAINT ON THE PREDICATE UNIT, measured and limiting: **the latch idiom is not uniform** -- at least four spellings coexist (struct `warned_*`, function-local `static int warned`, `static int w_warned/r_warned`, and others) -- so a predicate census needs the idiom normalised first, which is separate work and must not be smuggled in."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28684:   "state_note": "*** PASS 2 FOUND SIX CLEAN SWEEPS of the shipped 60-row detector (b78bb0f), the smallest ONE CHARACTER; the REPAIR is on disk, uncommitted: 82 rows (+8 IPL/MASK, +7 per-arm channels, +2 latch cardinality, +1 operand-swap, +4 odd-arm fall-through). Measured: part 1 nine mutants all killed at 78 (part1/driver.log); the builder's pass 3 killed all six sweeps at 78 and its 3b showed four fall-through TOTAL SWEEPS at 78 that its 3c kills 81/82 each at 82 (opus_pass3_20260903/); part 2 (M_IDATA_GENCTL vs 82) pending at the time of this note. PRETABLE.out written before table 2 (Section 0 records that table 1 had none). OWED before commit: part 2's sentinel, propagate dev_pcc2.c to build/ build-asan/ gxsec-build + two-part clearance, gate 14, CHANGELOG block from the logs, then PASS 3 (the eight other seats; the builder's pass 3 is one seat of it).  *** Superseded text, kept: FIXED (uncommitted at the time of writing; commit follows). Twelve width-guard blocks -> direction split: reads ACCEPT (the memcpy above the switch already served them; the tree's own header says wide access is LEGAL), writes DECLINE + per-instance 12-bit latch. DELETE is measurably wrong (big-endian compose: the low byte is the neighbour's lane) -- M_DELETE killed. The seven odd arms are OPERATOR-reachable (debugger put h, MEASURED) -- all twelve converted, M_OPKILL killed through that path. Detector 60 rows, gate 14 626/0, PREFIX 14/60, BASE 60/60, seven mutants zero sweeps. Design pass on 6 of 9 + one measurement (Opus x2 529, grok x2 CLI). Filed: pcc2c040kill (HIGH), pcc2counter8 (MED), pcc2widecompose. Owes pass 2.  2026-09-04 FIX REVIEW (owner directive): GPT-6 Astra (max) APPROVES the fix, DOES NOT CLOSE; Fable 5.1 GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Seven Astra findings confirmed from the tree; the main loop graded two as open floor cells -- the five HUH?-clear arms were then MEASURED HELD by announce-count rows (part 13 M_HUHV 249/251; the rbq rows strengthen the channel, L4 + coverage, not L2), the INT-set/count-0 cell awaits M_ZINT -> PASS 13 owed: 9 rows (242 -> 251), 7 mutants, gate, panel.  Astra dissents on the floor bound (Opus strength-t); the adjudicator adopts Fable's floor + the generating set.  PASS 13 PANEL COMPLETE 9/9 (2026-09-05 02:17-03:46).  RULING: PASS 14 OWED -- the class closure (three file-reading seats derived the same two gaps: the generating set one-sided at the two new INT cells; the INT axis not crossed with the other count), graded L1 (uncommitted detector material).  The pass-13 state is COMMITTED with the obligation OPEN (thirteen uncommitted passes were the larger exposure); pass 14 is drafted (fix_pcc2_p15.py: +18 rows -> 269, 8 mutants, PRETABLE_pass14, plus a pre-repair breach witness part14pre) and runs after the pcc2defaultkill fix ships (owner priority: critical first).\n\n*** THE TITLE'S \"TEN LIVE WIDTH KILLS\" IS STALE.  `b78bb0f` REMOVED ALL TEN.  Verified by me at\nHEAD, not taken from the seat. ***\n\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  2\n      :276  reassert_interrupts()          <- the C040 bit; that is [[pcc2c040kill]], a separate row\n      :380  pcctwo_interrupt_common()\n\n**Both are OUTSIDE the width guards.**  codex's count across the history is\n**13 before `b78bb0f`, 3 immediately after, 2 at HEAD**, and it read the guards directly: the commit\nconverted **all twelve**, so reads accept and wide writes decline.\n\n> **THE ROW STAYS OPEN AND ITS REASON CHANGES.**  `7076134` explicitly leaves pass 14 owed -- the new\n> timer interrupt-state cells lack the full generating set and the cross-product.  *Keeping it open\n> for that detector obligation is justified; describing ten width kills as LIVE is not,* and this is\n> a `high` row, so the overstatement is the kind that mis-ranks the whole queue.\n\n*This is the third row today whose headline count outlived the fix that removed it -- after\n`gdromkeyindex`'s \"five mutants score 32/32\" and `sh4iprunpinned`'s \"only four of ~20\".  A count in a\nTITLE is the least likely part of a row to be re-derived, because the title is what a reader uses to\ndecide whether to open the row at all.*\n\n*** THE CENSUS MOVED: `dev_pcc2.c` IS DOWN TO **ONE** `exit(1)`.  Re-derived 2026-09-08 with this\nrow's own deriver, after `#512` landed. ***\n\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  1\n    grep -nE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  :414\n\nThis row's note above enumerates TWO at HEAD -- `:276` in `reassert_interrupts()` (the C040 bit) and\n`:380` in `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`.  **`#512` removed the first**, and the second has moved to\n`:414` because that fix added lines above it.  *Both facts illustrate this row's own lesson: the\nline numbers decay and the DERIVER does not, which is why the deriver is recorded beside them.*\n\n**WHAT THE SURVIVOR IS, checked while verifying `#512`'s interrupt path rather than as a new hunt:**\n`pcctwo_interrupt_common()`'s `default:` arm kills on an interrupt line the switch does not case --\nit handles `PCC2V_SCSI`, `PCC2V_SCC_TX` and `PCC2V_SCC_RX`, and **`PCC2V_TIMER1` (0x09) and\n`PCC2V_TIMER2` (0x08) have no case**, although `reassert_interrupts()` names both when filling\n`cur_int_vec[]`.\n\n> **IT IS NOT GUEST-DRIVABLE TODAY, and the reason is construction rather than a guard.**\n> `interrupt->line` is fixed when a device is wired: `machine_mvme88k.c` connects only `clmpcc` and\n> `osiop` to pcc2, and the timers are serviced internally through `pending_timerN_interrupts`, never\n> through this callback.  **So the arm is a latent trap for a future device or machine that wires a\n> new line, not a live defect** -- which is why it is recorded here rather than filed as its own\n> round.\n\n*Checked against the ledger before writing: `pcctwo_interrupt_common` is already named in this row,\nso this is an update to an existing record and NOT a new row.  (Earlier this session I filed a\nduplicate that passed the gate and had to be withdrawn; the check is cheap and it worked here.)*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29153:   "note": "*** THREE SEPARATE ROUNDS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED FOR THE SAME IDEA AND ALL THREE USED PROCESS-GLOBAL STATE.  Found 2026-08-31 by re-deriving a census a seat had made with a narrower pattern. ***  **THE DERIVER, because two separate counts of this class have already been short:**  `grep -rn 'static int w = 0;|static int first = 1;|static char .*_used\\[' src/devices/*.c`  ->  **10 sites across 3 files** on 2026-08-31:  `dev_jazz.c` 2 (from `#179`), `dev_pcc2.c` 4 (from `#119`), `dev_sgi_re.c` 4 (upstream `rop_used`).  **THE DEFECT.**  A `static` inside a function is process-global, not per-instance.  Every one of these is a *warn-once* latch on a GUEST-DRIVABLE diagnostic, so **one guest access to one device instance permanently silences that diagnostic for every other instance of the same device in the machine** -- and for every later machine in the same process.  The house form is a per-instance bit in the device's own struct (`d->warned` plus `(once)` in the message text), which `#337`, `#460`, `#461`, `#497` and `#498` all use.  **THE INTENT IS RIGHT EVERYWHERE AND ONLY THE SCOPE IS WRONG.**  `#179`'s comment says *\"guest-reachable; ignore the write instead of fatal()-flooding host stdout\"*; `#119`'s says *\"was exit(1) -> warn-once + continue\"*.  Both are correct de-fatalisations that this project would make again.  **This row is about SCOPE, not disposition, and must not be read as reopening either round.**  *** HOW IT WAS FOUND IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: TWO CENSUSES OF THIS CLASS WERE BOTH SHORT, IN OPPOSITE WAYS. ***  A measuring seat censused it while answering an unrelated question and reported *\"4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside draw_primitive(), the tree's only such sites\"* -- true of its pattern (`static int first`/`_used[`) and false of the class, because `#119` and `#179` spell their latch `static int w = 0`.  Then a packet-fed seat on a DIFFERENT row challenged an `exit(` regex of mine as too narrow; the count it challenged did not move, **but chasing the four extra hits surfaced `#119`'s comments and hence the second file**.  *Neither seat was looking for this class.  A pattern-based census is only ever a census of its pattern, and the way out was two independent narrow greps disagreeing.*  **SCOPE NOTE for whoever takes this:** `dev_jazz.c` is one of the six paths in `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so a change there is SEC-only and does not owe an `est/` counterpart -- unlike the other two files.  **CONTROL a fix owes:** a per-instance latch must be shown to announce ONCE PER INSTANCE and not once per process -- i.e. a machine with two instances of the device, expecting two messages where the current code emits one.  That is the measurement that distinguishes the repair from a no-op rename.  *** THIS ROW'S OWN COUNT WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 23 SITES ACROSS 7 FILES, NOT 10 ACROSS 3.  THAT IS THE THIRD MISCOUNT OF THIS CLASS, AND THIS ONE IS MINE, USING THE DERIVER I WROTE WHILE TELLING OTHERS NOT TO TRANSCRIBE NUMBERS. ***  **The wider deriver:** `grep -rnE 'static int (w|warned|first) *= *[01];|static char [a-z_]+\\[' src/devices/*.c`  ->  **23 sites / 7 files**: `dev_osiop.c` **8**, `dev_pcc2.c` 5, `dev_sgi_re.c` 4, `dev_jazz.c` 2, `dev_pvr.c` 2, `dev_disk.c` 1, `dev_ps2_gs.c` 1.  **My deriver matched `static int w = 0` and `static int first = 1` and missed `static int warned = 0`, which is the DOMINANT spelling** -- it accounts for 16 of the 23.  `dev_osiop.c` alone has more sites than my entire filed count, and I never looked at that file.  *** THE LESSON IS ABOUT DERIVERS, NOT ABOUT THIS CLASS.  I filed this row saying \"record the deriver rather than the number, because this class has already been miscounted twice\" -- and the deriver I recorded was itself too narrow.  A deriver is only a census of its own pattern, exactly like the number it replaces; what it buys is that the NEXT reader can widen it, which is what happened here. ***  *Found because a `pcc2` seat's answer mentioned a `static int warned` site my census did not contain, and I re-ran the grep instead of assuming the seat meant one I already had.*  **`dev_osiop.c` should be looked at first by whoever takes this row**: 8 sites, several commented `#118/#119: was exit(1)`, so it is the same de-fatalisation campaign and the largest single population."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29164:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** THE WITNESS HAD A ROW THAT COULD NOT FAIL, AND A PASS-2 REVIEW OF THE ROUND FOUND IT.\nREPAIRED AND CONTROLLED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe rows tested `\"IOC_CONF: 0x100000000\" in text` -- a SUBSTRING -- and manufactured the displayed\n`got` from the boolean.  codex: *\"Append one literal 0 before the diagnostic's newline.  The message\nbecomes 0x1000000000, yet `kill width` still reports a green 0x100000000.\"*  `zero` had the mirror\nflaw (`0x0abc` contains `0x0`).\n\n> **That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape INSIDE THE WITNESS FOR THE DEFECT IT EXISTS TO PIN** --\n> and it survived my own review, a design review, and the run.  It was found only by asking a seat\n> what edit keeps the witness green.\n\n**REPAIRED** to parse the number and compare integers, reporting the value actually found.\n**CONTROLLED in both directions:**\n\n    fixed build, repaired oracle          6/6      kill width 0x100000000\n    codex's escape (TRAILING 0)           5/6      kill width 0x1000000000  RED\n\n*** AND MY FIRST CONTROL TESTED THE WRONG MUTANT, which is worth recording because it would have\nbeen a false proof. ***  I applied the extra `0` BEFORE the value (`0x0%\" PRIx64`), giving\n`0x0100000000` -- **numerically identical to `0x100000000`**, so the row passed correctly and proved\nnothing.  That is the carrier's own *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT* rule, case 1: the mutant\nis mis-built.  **The leading-zero form is a genuine EQUIVALENT mutant** and is recorded as such\nrather than as an escape.  The trailing-zero form is codex's, and it reds.\n\n*Artifacts: `probe_fix_newora_20260907.out`, `probe_mutant_trailzero_20260907.out`, and the\nmis-built `probe_mutant_extrazero_20260907.out` kept as the record of the equivalence.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/devices/dev_lca.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/devices/dev_lca.c\n                                   contains the `#506' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35029:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Evidence: regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt (GPT-6 Astra at max), which enumerated the population properly for the first time.  **This row exists so the next session reads the census instead of re-running it**, which has now happened twice.\n\n*** THE EARLIER FIGURES WERE RIGHT AND THEIR DESCRIPTION WAS WRONG: 370 and 240 count `exit(1)', NOT every `exit()'. ***  Re-derived by removing comments and string literals while preserving line positions, then locating the calls, with an independent character-state lexer returning exactly the same positions:\n\n    src/devices    263 literal `exit('   135 calls after lexical filtering\n    src/cpus       112                    74\n    src/machines    35                    34\n    TOTAL          410                   243   (240 of them exit(1))\n\nLOCATION SPLIT of those 243: 150 runtime handlers and helpers, 80 init/configuration, 13 CPU generators and a standalone experiment.  The debugger is outside that scope and adds three more.\n\nAND BEING INSIDE A RUNTIME FUNCTION IS NOT REACHABILITY.  The 150 split: **98 input-reachable through enabled in-tree wiring**; 33 dead, precluded, or not established reachable; 10 optional instrumentation; 6 unattached or disabled attachments; 2 intentional halt/reboot; 1 host RAM-config check misplaced in an access handler.\n\nOWNERSHIP is an OVERLAY, not a disjoint population -- 22 live exits and 3 others are actively owned (n6 holds eight footbridge sites; alphavmkill one; sgirekills five; lcaisalane and lcabadval eight between them; m88kexit two dead ones).  **A strict open/held filter leaves 83, of which seven belong to in-work LCA repairs, so THE UNOWNED REMAINDER IS 76.**\n\n*** AND ONE OF MY OWN EARLIER QUALIFICATIONS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE, corrected here: lacking a separate CHILD repair id does not mean lacking an open OWNING row.  `sgirekills' explicitly retains its five command exits.  Conversely an incidental mention in census prose is NOT a repair row. ***\n\nSIX E1 PREDICATES were identified -- a one-to-five-line change with an existing test carrier -- and ALL SIX ARE NOW FILED as their own rows: [[mk48nvramkill]], [[mapledmaaddr]], [[xscaleinten]], [[crimewidthkill]], [[vmedefaultkill]], [[fbctrlportkill]].  **This row therefore tracks the ~70 that remain, and they are E2/E3: real rounds, not quick wins.**  The heaviest concentrations are dev_osiop.c (14), cpu_arm_coproc.c (8 of which one is filed), dev_adb.c (7), dev_dreamcast_g2.c (6), dev_pvr.c and dev_igsfb.c (5 each).\n\nTHE ONE CONSTRAINT THAT GOVERNS EVERY DISPOSITION IN THIS BODY, and it is easy to forget: **memory_rw.c turns a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY.**  Returning zero from an ARM or SH device callback gives NO bus-error delivery, so the FAULT member is unavailable there and a round reaching for it owes the exception path first.  CPU translation and instruction handlers have their own direct mechanisms, which is a separate question."
