95: "rows": [
292:     "note": "Implemented and committed as f92e5a1 with its detector. Gate 2 PASS (122 checks) including the two new rows; the detector was negative-controlled in BOTH directions - no/no FAIL on the unfixed tree before the edit, yes/yes PASS after. Awaiting the panel's review of the actual diff.",
301:     "note": "NO-GO - and it was RIGHT on both counts. (1) The detector set os.environ in-process, so deleting the export from lib.sh left both rows GREEN - it covered the driver half and certified the other. (2) A relative $LOGDIR breaks: makedirs runs in the caller's directory, the opens run after chdir(IMAGES). Also caught that the message named a CLAUDE.md correction no hunk in this repo made, that 'neither half works alone' is overbroad, and that gate 2 would fail where the image directory is absent. ALL FIXED in 7cfa466.",
364:     "note": "GO-WITH-CHANGES (badge A: it authored this round's research design). MEASURED the detector vacuous for the export half - delete the export, both rows stay green - and MEASURED the relative-LOGDIR failure end to end (FileNotFoundError, no logs anywhere). Swept the containment row over 8000 start/duration pairs: minimum slack exactly 1.000 s, so it cannot false-fail from truncation or load. THREE RECORD FINDINGS: the filesystem-root mechanism is wrong (makedirs raises first); the CLAUDE.md sentence became a NON SEQUITUR when only its number was updated; and THE ROUND'S OWN NEGATIVE CONTROL POLLUTED /tmp/gxregress - the directory #422 exists to protect. All fixed in 7cfa466 and 0ad64b5.",
374:     "note": "PASS-WITH-GAPS. Ran the evidence rather than reading it: gate 2 PASS 131 checks twice, selftest 52 rows, invariant 685 steps, census 22/22 - no number differed from any claim. RE-KILLED BOTH R1 HALVES BY HAND (export deleted -> no/no FAIL; driver re-hardcoded -> no/no FAIL). Probed the census's honesty: an unwritable workdir crashes visibly rather than degrading, and the identity check was negative-controlled to FAIL on an inflated count. Found independently, before my fix landed: the rmtree hazard, the identity tautology, the exact-count row and the false 'sum idiom gone' claim. TWO GAPS REMAIN (now filed as rows): deleting gate 2's whole absorb block is INVISIBLE because its guards live inside it, and NO CROSS-CONSUMER CONTROL EXISTS for the driver/grader seam. Also: an interrupted census leaves its workdir (cleanup not in a finally), and BOOT_WALL=0.0 from an instant-exit rig reds containment - a false red, only on an already-failing rig. Unverified until the 08-22 battery: gate 5's new rows on real rigs, real BOOT_WALL magnitudes, gate 6 grading logs from the changed driver.",
383:     "note": "CLOSED AS ALREADY-COMPLETE, and verified before closing rather than taken from the hold. Nobody proposed dropping these -- the adjudicator found them while asked what should be closed that no seat had raised, and its point stands: FINISHED WORK HAD SAT OPEN FOR FIVE DAYS.\n\nChecked here independently: d1's $LOGDIR handling is at drive_guest.py:197-203, and the comment shows the empty-variable subtlety was handled deliberately (the `or` form matches lib.sh's own ${LOGDIR:-...}, which also treats empty as unset). a4's BOOT_WALL is at drive_guest.py:466, graded by a4-tagged rows at gate_crossfamily.sh:128-129 which EXPLICITLY FORBID a magnitude threshold -- 'a row of the form BOOT_WALL <= <constant> is the load-sensitive' shape this project has been burned by.\n\nThe adjudicator asked for one more check before closing: that the 'GAPS' in the PASS-WITH-GAPS regress verdict were FILED as rows rather than lost. They were -- xfamscope, optrow, constblind, seam and gateflr are all in the ledger, and xfamscope has since been measured ALREADY-RESOLVED by the a4/BOOT_WALL rewrite of the very lines it named. A gap that became its own row and then dissolved is the system working.",
596:     "note": "Implemented and committed as f92e5a1 with its detector. Gate 2 PASS (122 checks) including the two new rows; the detector was negative-controlled in BOTH directions - no/no FAIL on the unfixed tree before the edit, yes/yes PASS after. Awaiting the panel's review of the actual diff.",
605:     "note": "NO-GO - and it was RIGHT on both counts. (1) The detector set os.environ in-process, so deleting the export from lib.sh left both rows GREEN - it covered the driver half and certified the other. (2) A relative $LOGDIR breaks: makedirs runs in the caller's directory, the opens run after chdir(IMAGES). Also caught that the message named a CLAUDE.md correction no hunk in this repo made, that 'neither half works alone' is overbroad, and that gate 2 would fail where the image directory is absent. ALL FIXED in 7cfa466.",
668:     "note": "GO-WITH-CHANGES (badge A: it authored this round's research design). MEASURED the detector vacuous for the export half - delete the export, both rows stay green - and MEASURED the relative-LOGDIR failure end to end (FileNotFoundError, no logs anywhere). Swept the containment row over 8000 start/duration pairs: minimum slack exactly 1.000 s, so it cannot false-fail from truncation or load. THREE RECORD FINDINGS: the filesystem-root mechanism is wrong (makedirs raises first); the CLAUDE.md sentence became a NON SEQUITUR when only its number was updated; and THE ROUND'S OWN NEGATIVE CONTROL POLLUTED /tmp/gxregress - the directory #422 exists to protect. All fixed in 7cfa466 and 0ad64b5.",
678:     "note": "PASS-WITH-GAPS. Ran the evidence rather than reading it: gate 2 PASS 131 checks twice, selftest 52 rows, invariant 685 steps, census 22/22 - no number differed from any claim. RE-KILLED BOTH R1 HALVES BY HAND (export deleted -> no/no FAIL; driver re-hardcoded -> no/no FAIL). Probed the census's honesty: an unwritable workdir crashes visibly rather than degrading, and the identity check was negative-controlled to FAIL on an inflated count. Found independently, before my fix landed: the rmtree hazard, the identity tautology, the exact-count row and the false 'sum idiom gone' claim. TWO GAPS REMAIN (now filed as rows): deleting gate 2's whole absorb block is INVISIBLE because its guards live inside it, and NO CROSS-CONSUMER CONTROL EXISTS for the driver/grader seam. Also: an interrupted census leaves its workdir (cleanup not in a finally), and BOOT_WALL=0.0 from an instant-exit rig reds containment - a false red, only on an already-failing rig. Unverified until the 08-22 battery: gate 5's new rows on real rigs, real BOOT_WALL magnitudes, gate 6 grading logs from the changed driver.",
687:     "note": "CLOSED AS ALREADY-COMPLETE, and verified before closing rather than taken from the hold. Nobody proposed dropping these -- the adjudicator found them while asked what should be closed that no seat had raised, and its point stands: FINISHED WORK HAD SAT OPEN FOR FIVE DAYS.\n\nChecked here independently: d1's $LOGDIR handling is at drive_guest.py:197-203, and the comment shows the empty-variable subtlety was handled deliberately (the `or` form matches lib.sh's own ${LOGDIR:-...}, which also treats empty as unset). a4's BOOT_WALL is at drive_guest.py:466, graded by a4-tagged rows at gate_crossfamily.sh:128-129 which EXPLICITLY FORBID a magnitude threshold -- 'a row of the form BOOT_WALL <= <constant> is the load-sensitive' shape this project has been burned by.\n\nThe adjudicator asked for one more check before closing: that the 'GAPS' in the PASS-WITH-GAPS regress verdict were FILED as rows rather than lost. They were -- xfamscope, optrow, constblind, seam and gateflr are all in the ledger, and xfamscope has since been measured ALREADY-RESOLVED by the a4/BOOT_WALL rewrite of the very lines it named. A gap that became its own row and then dissolved is the system working.",
693:   "witness": "harness:the BOOT_WALL presence-and-containment rows, swept over 8000 start/duration pairs with minimum slack exactly 1.000 s, so they cannot false-fail from truncation or load"
703:    "note": "Statement of record, amended by the R2 census: the selftest is invoked by zero gates AND its helper returns body+tail so 27 of 29 rows compare the SUM - a mutant that only MOVES text between the halves is invisible. MEASURED against 22 mutants: the shipped test kills 17 and misses 5, every one of them non-equivalent with an executed witness. The strengthened design (nine added rows pinning the two halves separately, plus a hold-contract invariant checked on all 1216 calls) kills 22 of 22. Denominator note: the historical 13-of-18 figure belongs to GATE rows, not to this file. R2 RESEARCH CLOSURE: both designs adopted - the invariants catch the CLASS, the section-K rows catch the edges the invariants cannot (the hold bound still satisfies the length test; the regex-literal mutant never touches the tail). The contested conversion was settled by execution: 2,575 parser steps, zero violations of either invariant on the shipped parser. IMPLEMENTED (81506bd): 52 rows; census committed as absorb_census.py; 22/22 killed."
738:     "note": "REAL — selftest_absorb.py (29 rows, shipped split_stream) is referenced by zero gates; run.sh's 16-gate list omits it",
756:     "note": "REAL — selftest_absorb.py exists in regress/ (159 lines, 29 test rows) but is called by zero regress/*.sh gate scripts.",
801:     "note": "REAL - RAN the selftest: 29 rows, 0 failures, PASS (29 is a RUNTIME count; 22 static check( sites, the rest are loops - a static count will look like a discrepancy and is not one). NAMES A SURVIVOR: dropping the newline clause at drive_guest.py:186 passes all 29 rows; proven non-equivalent by execution. Cause: feed() returns body+tail and 27 rows compare the SUM, so a mutant that only MOVES text between them is invisible.",
810:     "note": "Make gate 2 enforce a PARTITION-SENSITIVE, CANARY-BACKED 18-mutant parser selftest - it frames the whole round as one property (the partition between body and tail) rather than a list of rows.",
819:     "note": "Pin body and tail separately on ALL newline-terminated and multi-chunk rows, not only the held-line ones.",
828:     "note": "Pin body and tail separately on every hold/release row; wire into gate_offline.sh with an IN-FILE mutant positive control; re-run the census from per-mutant scratch copies. Names its rejections: gate_hygiene (a static log grader - execution breaks its idiom), asserting body alone (a duplicate-to-both-halves mutant passes), converting all 27 sum rows (churn, zero extra kills), touching drive_guest.py (shipped behaviour is correct), folding into selftest_mutation.sh (couples two subjects to one gate token). AND THE RULE THAT MATTERS: the census must report 18 NAMED mutants, never 'N of N killed' - no denominator reframing.",
864:     "note": "packet: strengthen selftest_absorb.py with body/tail-pinning rows; wire into gate_offline.sh with a positive-control substep.",
873:     "note": "CENSUS BUILT AND RUN, not designed on paper: 22 mutants across seven axes, applied to COPIES with each substitution asserted to apply exactly once (else scored MALFORMED, never a survivor). SHIPPED TEST: 17 killed, 5 SURVIVED, 0 malformed - and all five are NON-EQUIVALENT with executed witnesses, so none can be retired. Beyond the named one: dropping the i != -1 guard holds the last character for ever; < -> <= at the hold bound (the existing row uses 400, far past the edge); rest.rfind -> tail.rfind leaks a second record unstripped; dropping the ' instrs' literal invents a count from '[ 100 frobs; p]'. STRENGTHENED DESIGN MEASURED: 38 rows, 22 of 22 killed, 88 ms. CORRECTS THE BRIEF: the historical 13-of-18 figure is about GATE rows, not this file - a different denominator that the closure and my brief both conflated.",
882:     "note": "Reframes the fix as TWO INVARIANTS rather than rows: the hold shape after every step, and finality (a newline-terminated input must leave the tail empty) - which lets every sum row convert to a pair, so THE BLIND IDIOM STOPS EXISTING. Its positive control is THE NAMED SURVIVOR MUTANT applied to copies, which proves the row KILLS the mutant where an in-process wrong-expectation row only proves the reporter can print FAIL. And it wrote THE DENOMINATOR RULE: attempted=killed+survived+faults, self-checked, every survivor named with a closed-vocabulary disposition, a crash is a FAULT never a detection, and no ratio may use a denominator other than attempted.",
891:     "note": "Implemented and committed as 81506bd. MEASURED: the selftest is 52 rows / 0 failures on the shipped parser, and the committed census now reports 22 of 22 killed, 0 survived, 0 faults, with its attempted=killed+survived+faults identity self-checked. Gate 2 PASS at 131 checks (was 122). THE GATE CAUGHT ONE OF MY OWN ROWS: the control asserted the kill came from exactly one named row, but the mutant strands every bracket-carrying line and fails three - now a floor, with the reason recorded. Awaiting the panel's review of the diff.",
927:     "note": "GO-WITH-CHANGES - independently found the leftover sum rows AND the rmtree hazard.",
936:     "note": "NO-GO - claimed the gate wants 3 body-landing rows while the selftest emits 2, so the PASS 'cannot be true'. MEASURED WRONG: the file emits 3. It counted from a partial hunk - the honest limit of a seat that cannot open the file.",
973:     "note": "PASS-WITH-GAPS. Ran the evidence rather than reading it: gate 2 PASS 131 checks twice, selftest 52 rows, invariant 685 steps, census 22/22 - no number differed from any claim. RE-KILLED BOTH R1 HALVES BY HAND (export deleted -> no/no FAIL; driver re-hardcoded -> no/no FAIL). Probed the census's honesty: an unwritable workdir crashes visibly rather than degrading, and the identity check was negative-controlled to FAIL on an inflated count. Found independently, before my fix landed: the rmtree hazard, the identity tautology, the exact-count row and the false 'sum idiom gone' claim. TWO GAPS REMAIN (now filed as rows): deleting gate 2's whole absorb block is INVISIBLE because its guards live inside it, and NO CROSS-CONSUMER CONTROL EXISTS for the driver/grader seam. Also: an interrupted census leaves its workdir (cleanup not in a finally), and BOOT_WALL=0.0 from an instant-exit rig reds containment - a false red, only on an already-failing rig. Unverified until the 08-22 battery: gate 5's new rows on real rigs, real BOOT_WALL magnitudes, gate 6 grading logs from the changed driver.",
988:    "note": "Statement of record: gate 5 grades the constants the driver PRINTS, not the ones it USES; and the zero branch is tested before the luna88k branch, so BUDGET=0 prints a green row while removing the only work ceiling. MANUAL - the mutants are predicted, not executed, so build them and watch the rows stay green BEFORE repairing. R3 RESEARCH CLOSURE: the hole is OBSERVED (both mutants green, literal lines banked). THE MAJORITY DESIGN IS REFUTED - shared locals cannot kill an argument-expression mutant, because a local proves what the caller INTENDED to pass, not what the callee RECEIVED. Chosen: record the callee's formal parameters and print those; order the calibrated-rig case first and make it RED; factor the classifier into lib.sh so the branch is reachable offline; and ship a STRADDLE detector that reads the printed budget from the driver's own output and proves N-1 does not stop while N+1 does."
1167:     "note": "THE HOLE OBSERVED, NOT PREDICTED - the round's disposition demanded it. A2: a luna88k output with BUDGET=0 printed 'ok luna88k: budget is deliberately absent (uncalibrated rig)' and PASS 11/11 while asserting something false about a 12 G rig; negative- controlled BOTH ways (landisk's legitimate zero stays green under both orders). A1: control and mutant byte-identical apart from telemetry, rows PASS 11/11, and the mutation is LIVE - control stops at REASON=BUDGET NINSTRS=13e9 while the mutant runs to REASON=STALLED NINSTRS=20e9, both printing BUDGET=12000000000. Also named the trap in the naive repair (.get('stall',120) on both paths would print a PASSING 120 where today it prints a FAILING 0) and recorded that its own stub was at fault for two early reds - an uncorrected stub would have UNDERSTATED the hole.",
1259:     "note": "GO-WITH-CHANGES (badge A). FOUND THE DEFECT THAT SURVIVED THE FIRST FOLLOW-UP, and it is this project's own vacuity class committed BY the round closing that class: dropping the .get defaults DECAPITATED gate 2's OTHER driver selftest, because a THIRD rig dict (selftest_logdir's) declared neither key - and both its rows kept passing, since the log files open BEFORE the raise. Caught it by TIMING: 1.573 s and 15 driver lines became 0.062 s and one, still PASS. Also: the straddle cannot see a mutant that scales BOTH the print and the comparison - only the literal config-pin row can, so deriving the over-leg from the printed value REMOVED a redundancy; `>` -> `>=` scores zero red rows; and my orphan-child justification was UNMEASURED (it measured zero survivors both ways). Confirmed `used` is populated on every path, and that /tmp/gxregress was untouched.",
1277:    "note": "REVIEWED AND FOLLOWED UP: 863d238 (round) + b9daca1 (records) + cdc4849 (follow-up). Eight of nine seats answered; none found anything wrong with the three shipped fixes -- the domain clamp, the catch-up bound, the RTC narrowing and the above-the-early-out placement were each confirmed by every seat that could trace them. EVERY DEFECT FOUND WAS IN THE INSTRUMENT OR THE RECORDS. Detector 14 -> 24 rows; gate 2 148 -> 157 checks; census 13 mutants, 13 killed, 0 survived, 0 faults. The generalisable finding: reading a constant from the header defeats transcription drift AND creates blindness to the constant itself, so a row must also assert an absolute consequence. Regression review batched to Sunday night per P12.  [A: the diff under review was mine, disclosed.]"
1456:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX. Two findings nobody else had in full: the cap and the domain are INCONSISTENT (the domain admits INT_MAX but cap x base can only ever serve 68,157,440 ticks/s, so above that the debt grows forever and 'delivered by the signals that follow' is untrue), and it is REACHABLE through cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1: 166.56 MHz on Algor, 360 MHz on VoCore. So R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE. Also found the gate floor at 13 vs 14 rows, the constant-deletion mutant, and interval=0.5/freq sailing over every floor. Sandbox denied execution, so MANUAL not CONFIRMED, and it said so.",
1538:     "ev": "_scratchpad/battery_20260816.log (16/16, 1043 rows, 0 skipped)",
1539:     "note": "REGRESS_PASS, 16/16, 0 failed, 0 SKIPPED, 1,043 ok rows -- and the arithmetic was verified: 1,043 is exactly the sum of the sixteen per-gate counts, so no phantom rows. The chain that lets offline rows speak for rig binaries is CLOSED: the diffs compile SEC repo source, gate 1 proves both build trees byte-synced to SEC, the rigs run the build binaries. THE VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY DEFLATIONARY: this green licenses 'every R1-R9 detector still passes against today's source, and four rigs still boot'. It does NOT license 'every R1-R9 fix is still present' -- #429 and #431 are both revertible under a green battery -- and it does NOT license 'the new detectors can still fail', because gate 3 is the only failability control and all seven of its mutants target float_emul.c, none of the five harnesses R4-R9 added. COI disclosed by the seat itself: the R1-R9 commits carry its own Co-Authored-By, so this is same-model review of same-model work (A,s), and it weighted construction-proofs over judgement accordingly.  ROUND-SPECIFIC: Same split as tfreq: the timer half is well covered, the dev_rtc half is not. CONFIRMED."
1546:     "note": "CLOSED. Same round as tfreq: identical closure note, identical commits, identical hold.\n\nThese three were active only because nobody flipped the state -- they are ONE ROUND WEARING THREE NAMES, carrying identical closure notes, the same three commits and the same hold. The differential against upstream 39748e3 settles that the defect was real (5 of 12 rows red pre-fix, so the detector is not vacuous) and that it is gone.",
1551:   "witness": "harness:offline differential of the SAME driver against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c` -- 12 rows on t->interval == 1.0/t->freq, 0 fails on shipped, 5 fails on upstream. NOT a rung-2-4 witness and must not be read as one: it is a rung-1 direct-call detector, which the ladder says is NEVER a reproduction. It proves the row was real (not vacuous) and that the defect is gone; it does not prove the path was guest-reachable -- and separately grep shows t->freq is read NOWHERE outside timer.c, so the guest could never have observed it."
1562:    "note": "REVIEWED AND FOLLOWED UP: 863d238 (round) + b9daca1 (records) + cdc4849 (follow-up). Eight of nine seats answered; none found anything wrong with the three shipped fixes -- the domain clamp, the catch-up bound, the RTC narrowing and the above-the-early-out placement were each confirmed by every seat that could trace them. EVERY DEFECT FOUND WAS IN THE INSTRUMENT OR THE RECORDS. Detector 14 -> 24 rows; gate 2 148 -> 157 checks; census 13 mutants, 13 killed, 0 survived, 0 faults. The generalisable finding: reading a constant from the header defeats transcription drift AND creates blindness to the constant itself, so a row must also assert an absolute consequence. Regression review batched to Sunday night per P12.  [A: the diff under review was mine, disclosed.]"
