1954:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX. Two findings nobody else had in full: the cap and the domain are INCONSISTENT (the domain admits INT_MAX but cap x base can only ever serve 68,157,440 ticks/s, so above that the debt grows forever and 'delivered by the signals that follow' is untrue), and it is REACHABLE through cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1: 166.56 MHz on Algor, 360 MHz on VoCore. So R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE. Also found the gate floor at 13 vs 14 rows, the constant-deletion mutant, and interval=0.5/freq sailing over every floor. Sandbox denied execution, so MANUAL not CONFIRMED, and it said so.",
2036:     "ev": "_scratchpad/battery_20260816.log (16/16, 1043 rows, 0 skipped)",
2037:     "note": "REGRESS_PASS, 16/16, 0 failed, 0 SKIPPED, 1,043 ok rows -- and the arithmetic was verified: 1,043 is exactly the sum of the sixteen per-gate counts, so no phantom rows. The chain that lets offline rows speak for rig binaries is CLOSED: the diffs compile SEC repo source, gate 1 proves both build trees byte-synced to SEC, the rigs run the build binaries. THE VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY DEFLATIONARY: this green licenses 'every R1-R9 detector still passes against today's source, and four rigs still boot'. It does NOT license 'every R1-R9 fix is still present' -- #429 and #431 are both revertible under a green battery -- and it does NOT license 'the new detectors can still fail', because gate 3 is the only failability control and all seven of its mutants target float_emul.c, none of the five harnesses R4-R9 added. COI disclosed by the seat itself: the R1-R9 commits carry its own Co-Authored-By, so this is same-model review of same-model work (A,s), and it weighted construction-proofs over judgement accordingly.  ROUND-SPECIFIC: R4's TIMER half is among the BEST-covered code in gate 2 -- 24 rows against the real timer.c, constants asserted as absolute consequences, a revert flips rows directly. CONFIRMED. Its RTC half has NOTHING (see rtcgate). Calling 'R4' weak would smear a strong detector; calling it strong would bury a hole. The unit of coverage is the HUNK."
2071:     "note": "THE LAST THREE UNPANELLED ROWS -- the final panel of a nine-panel campaign that took the backlog from 44 open single-seat rows to zero.\n\n*** THE SEATS SPLIT ON `tfreq` IN THE EXACT SHAPE THE BRIEF WARNED ABOUT, AND THE MEASUREMENT SETTLED IT. ***  agy and deepseek said tfreq is ALREADY FIXED and should be closed, not worked; minimax said do it FIRST, as a FIX+DETECTOR round. A direct contradiction about whether a defect still exists -- so it was CHECKED rather than voted.\n\nMEASURED, and it is decisive. BOTH write paths clamp before storing:\n  timer.c:130-136  '#427: clamp before anything is stored, so freq and interval agree' --\n                   freq = timer_clamp_freq(...) THEN newtimer->freq = freq.\n  timer_update_frequency()  '#427/#429: CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store. The order is load-bearing and the shipped order was wrong twice over. THE OLD CODE STORED THE RAW VALUE INTO t->freq AND CLAMPED ONLY THE LOCAL, SO t->freq NAMED A RATE THE TIMER DID NOT RUN AT.'\nThat last sentence IS tfreq's title, in the source, marked fixed. tfreq is FIXED; its row is `active` with commits 863d238/b9daca1/cdc4849 and its hold says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night'. It is awaiting a BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, not open work.\n\nTHE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE PREDICTED THIS EXACTLY: the two seats that could OPEN THE FILE got it right; the packet-fed seat, reasoning from the row's text alone, would have sent a round at an already-closed defect. That is the fourth time in one night that a row's own text pointed the wrong way, and the second time a packet seat was overruled by one grep.\n\nes438: DROP AS A DEFECT ROW -- three seats (agy, deepseek, minimax), and agy's version is the useful one: CLOSE it as a completed review record while SPLITTING OUT the luna fuse-write defect it contains. A process record is not a defect row, but a real defect buried inside one must not be dropped with its container.\n\nrtcnarrow: deepseek would do it first. It is the design item filed earlier tonight, whose own three-device premise was already re-derived and largely dissolved.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 9.5 KB, grok 9.8 KB, kimi 81.4 KB, glm 8.4 KB, deepseek 4.0 KB, minimax 12.3 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, ninth consecutive wall, proven arithmetically again: '21348b = brief 20660b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: *** MEASURED ALREADY FIXED -- close it, do not work it. ***  Two seats said so, one said do it FIRST, and the source settles it: both timer.c write paths clamp BEFORE storing, and timer_update_frequency's own comment states this row's title as the defect it fixed. The row is `active` with commits and its hold already says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night' -- so it is awaiting its BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, which is a queue position, not open work. NOT CLOSED HERE: codex-held, and a closure needs the full panel.",
2098:     "note": "WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.\n\nITS VERDICT: VERDICT: Do `tfreq` first, dropping `rtcnarrow` as filed and `es438` from the open defect queue.\n\nCAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel's own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a '+1' mutant that 'admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green' -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: 'do tfreq first' (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 'CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store'), 'mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness' (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and 'drop constblind' (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier).",
2107:     "note": "*** CLOSE IT -- MEASURED FIXED, AND MEASURED TO HAVE HAD NIL GUEST HARM EVEN WHEN BROKEN. ***  Reading the fixed code cannot prove the row was ever a defect, so the seat built a discriminator on one property (t->interval == 1.0/t->freq) and compiled the SAME DRIVER against shipped timer.c and against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c`:\n    shipped 020a672  12 rows, 0 fails GREEN     upstream 39748e3  5 fails RED\nThree things a reading could not establish: (1) the row WAS real -- 5 of 12 go red pre-fix, so not vacuous; (2) *** timer_add WAS NEVER THE DEFECTIVE PATH *** -- upstream already clamped before storing there, and only timer_update_frequency stored raw, so the row's title ('the timer core') names a function too broadly; (3) grep -rn '->freq' src/ outside timer.c returns NOTHING -- t->freq is read only by the early-out inside timer_update_frequency itself, which pre-fix held raw-vs-raw, so idempotence survived too.\n\n*** AND THE BRIEF HEADER I WROTE IS A WRONG RECORD THAT SPLIT THE PANEL. ***  It says 'the LAST THREE unpanelled rows, each filed by ONE seat, none ever panelled.' The ledger gives tfreq 29 ENTRIES ACROSS ALL FOUR PHASES FROM ALL NINE SEATS, and the brief's own body quotes fourteen of them. The real criterion was 'thin ASSESS cell', not 'never panelled'. Seats trusting the header saw an open unreviewed defect; seats reading the body saw a finished round. Both were reading the same packet.\n\ntfreq/n2/n4 carry IDENTICAL closure notes, the same three commits (863d238 b9daca1 cdc4849) and the same hold. They are ONE ROUND WEARING THREE NAMES, active only because nobody flipped the state.\n\n---\n*** PHASE CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this answer was first filed under `review` and belongs in `assess`. ***  The cluster brief it answers asked the assess questionnaire -- rank, drop/merge, fix-vs-document-vs-detector, reachable-vs-theoretical, smallest harm, witnessable at what rung, what the filing seats missed -- and none of that is a diff review. Fourteen rows carried the same mis-filing.\n\nIt was caught by precommit section H, which reported something else: 'tfreq CLOSED-WHILE-HELD, a round may not close while a stage awaits a seat.' True as stated, and the cause was not a missing seat -- opus5 HAD answered, at 1628 bytes, into the wrong phase. A gate that names a real problem by the wrong mechanism is still the gate earning its keep; the alternative was closing fourteen rows over holds that looked live.",
2109:     "ev": "differential vs 39748e3:src/core/timer.c, 12 rows"
2116:     "note": "CLOSED. Measured fixed AND measured to have had nil guest harm even when broken.\n\nThese three were active only because nobody flipped the state -- they are ONE ROUND WEARING THREE NAMES, carrying identical closure notes, the same three commits and the same hold. The differential against upstream 39748e3 settles that the defect was real (5 of 12 rows red pre-fix, so the detector is not vacuous) and that it is gone.",
2121:   "witness": "harness:offline differential of the SAME driver against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c` -- 12 rows on t->interval == 1.0/t->freq, 0 fails on shipped, 5 fails on upstream. NOT a rung-2-4 witness and must not be read as one: it is a rung-1 direct-call detector, which the ladder says is NEVER a reproduction. It proves the row was real (not vacuous) and that the defect is gone; it does not prove the path was guest-reachable -- and separately grep shows t->freq is read NOWHERE outside timer.c, so the guest could never have observed it."
2131:    "note": "SHIPPED as 2458cfb. Pass 2 found THREE defects in the fix and one vacuous row, all fixed before the commit: the split granule was 4 KB where ARM/SH can map at 1 KB; splitting broke #244's whole-buffer zero promise on a read that faults before its last chunk; `offset + len` could wrap; and the row certifying 'a loop, not recursion' passed under the recursion mutant because gcc eliminates the tail call at -O2. MEASURED: differential 23 rows / 0 failures, 15 of 23 red against the pre-change file; gate 2 PASS at 171 checks (was 157); builds across all eight flavours including the cpu_alpha.c double-inclusion; gate_mips PASS 6/6 with pmax 15/15 and arc 13/13 to uid=0(root) on freshly built binaries. The design fork was settled by MEASUREMENT over a 5-to-1 majority, and the dissenting option's failing case is now a permanent row.  [A: the diff was mine.]"
2328:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX, and it FUZZED rather than read -- 40,000 reads and 20,000 writes against a per-byte reference over a random page PERMUTATION, 0 divergences, with the pre-change file as a negative control at 39,388. That is the strongest single piece of evidence this round has. It then found what the rows could not see: a one-line off-by-one (`granule - offset + 1`) that passed all 21 rows while diverging 1,089 times, because no row had a tail of exactly one byte; and -- the sharpest finding -- that the 'it is a loop' row was VACUOUS at -O2, because gcc 15.2.1 DOES eliminate the tail call, the opposite of what the round had recorded. Disclosed its own conflict of interest (it designed the change) and built a negative control before trusting any green. Badge A.",
2356:     "note": "REGRESS_PASS, 16/16, 0 failed, 0 SKIPPED, 1,043 ok rows -- and the arithmetic was verified: 1,043 is exactly the sum of the sixteen per-gate counts, so no phantom rows. The chain that lets offline rows speak for rig binaries is CLOSED: the diffs compile SEC repo source, gate 1 proves both build trees byte-synced to SEC, the rigs run the build binaries. THE VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY DEFLATIONARY: this green licenses 'every R1-R9 detector still passes against today's source, and four rigs still boot'. It does NOT license 'every R1-R9 fix is still present' -- #429 and #431 are both revertible under a green battery -- and it does NOT license 'the new detectors can still fail', because gate 3 is the only failability control and all seven of its mutants target float_emul.c, none of the five harnesses R4-R9 added. COI disclosed by the seat itself: the R1-R9 commits carry its own Co-Authored-By, so this is same-model review of same-model work (A,s), and it weighted construction-proofs over judgement accordingly.  ROUND-SPECIFIC: R7 is THE BEST-DEFENDED ROUND OF THE NINE: three independent layers all present in this log -- the 25-row differential, m8online's NINE REAL GUEST INSTRUCTIONS through the real address decode in gate 11 (values, not survival: IDR reads 0x00a90000 through a guest load), and the luna88k boot. CONFIRMED.  ATTRIBUTED LATE, and batched: this row's commit 2458cfb is R5, which the batched brief `_scratchpad/brief_fable_regress_R1R9.md` names by content in its scope table, so the pass did cover it -- but the entry landed on the round rows and never on the two BUG rows for the same commit.  Found by `check_fable_queue.py` on its first run."
