2660:     "note": "REAL — both are data-corruption bugs, but reachability depends on overlay/short-read usage",
2927:   "witness": "detector:regress/diff_diskimage_io.c -- OWNER-ADOPTED TERM, 2026-08-27.  The fix shipped with a MEASURED detector and a kill table, and no guest-path witness.  `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG: it ranks below every access witness and does NOT discharge the rung-3 ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires.  Before this term the row had to be written `none`, which asserts DOCUMENTED-ONLY and was false about a round that shipped a fix.  *** THIS FIELD CITED `regress/diff_fb_clear.c`, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST AND NEVER DID. Corrected 2026-08-27 by a sibling grep. ***  The real detector is `regress/diff_diskimage_io.c` (its section is `section_overlay_unaligned`, tagged b115 in the file).  Found while correcting `gdromexitsix`, whose `probe:` field cited two scratchpad scripts that are also gone -- the adjudicating seat pointed out that the grep-for-siblings rule had been skipped, and doing it turned up these two.  **The `detector:` term was owner-adopted on 2026-08-27, used on three rows that day, and TWO of the three cited a file that does not exist** -- a plausible-looking filename written from intent rather than from the tree.",
2928:   "state_note": "*** TWO CORRECTIONS OF MINE, BOTH SERIOUS, FROM THE OPUS PASS-2 SEAT. ***\n\n*** 1. FLOOR CLAUSE (d) WAS BREACHED AND I HAD ARGUED THE WRONG CLAUSE. *** The fix INTRODUCES the predicate `len > remain`, and clause (d) says weaken it to the least-restrictive form that still READS like the same check. **Two such mutants passed all six rows:**\n```\n  len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE ? remain : len     bound relaxed\n  remain = inblock ? 1 : OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE   remainder faked\n```\nBoth survived because the section only produced **inblock in {0,511} and len > 512.**\n\n**AND THE UNCOVERED CALLER IS REAL, AND THIS ROUND'S OWN CENSUS HAD NAMED IT:** `bootblock_iso9660.c:281-282` reads **256 bytes** at a `dirofs` snapped only to 2048 (`:275-276`) -- arbitrary mod 512. *The census named the arm and the rows did not cover it.*\n\n**I had argued L1 (*these rows are not vacuous*) and declined a fixture change. That was the WRONG CLAUSE.** This is (d)/L2, which the carrier says to replace IN-ROUND however late it is found. Fixed in-round.\n\n**ONE ROW KILLS BOTH, and the kill is MEASURED, not reasoned:**\n```\n  arm        short-unaligned row (510+3, want 2)   total\n  fixed      ok                                    53 rows, 0 failures\n  pristine   FAIL                                  2 failures\n  relaxed    FAIL   <- was GREEN before this row   1 failure\n  faked      FAIL   <- was GREEN before this row   1 failure\n```\n\n*** 2. I MISREAD MY OWN PANEL AND REPORTED IT AS THREE RATIFICATIONS. *** I wrote *'three seats have already said GO/RATIFY/sound'* -- into the brief I then handed a later seat. **The panel was NOT unanimous:** codex **GO WITH CHANGES**; grok **'PRODUCT DIFF IS SOUND; DETECTOR IS NOT DONE'**; minimax **'NO RATIFICATION'**, stated twice; the kimi substitute challenged the severity framing. **Only agy and deepseek ratified.**\n\n> **This is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape with an extra step, and worse than the usual form: I did not merely fail to read three dissents -- I propagated the misreading into a brief, so a later seat was told the panel agreed.** The seats that said DETECTOR IS NOT DONE were RIGHT, and their finding is exactly the clause-(d) breach above.\n\n*The lesson is the carrier's own, which I quoted at other rows today and did not apply here: **read the BODY of every seat whose verdict merely agrees** -- and, it turns out, of every seat whose verdict I have not actually read at all.*\n\n*** THE L1 QUESTION ON THE GEOMETRY GAP, CONSIDERED AND ANSWERED -- recorded so the next round does not re-argue it. ***\n\n**The case FOR fixing it in-round (L1):** the detector is still UNCOMMITTED, and L1 licenses in-round repair of *the row is part of the deliverable still being built... a row measured vacuous before commit is unfinished, not a reopening*. The tree also carries a MEASURED precedent that single-overlay fixtures are inadequate -- `diff_diskimage_sync.c:570`: *\"A SECOND OVERLAY IS WHAT MAKES THE ROW WORK, and it is not decoration... MEASURED: a single-overlay version of this row caught the [nothing]\"*. And a second overlay would strengthen EVERY row rather than adding one.\n\n*** THE CASE AGAINST, WHICH WINS: L1 IS ABOUT VACUITY, AND THESE ROWS ARE NOT VACUOUS. *** They have a measured kill table -- they redden on the pre-fix build and on the escaping mutant, and go green on the fix. The vacuity floor asks whether a detector fails on mutants OF THE FIX. **A layer-selection bug is not a mutant of this fix; it is a DIFFERENT DEFECT** that this fixture happens to be unable to see. Calling that L1 would make L1 mean *any adjacent class a detector does not cover*, which is unbounded -- and unbounded in-round strengthening is precisely what `#392` was.\n\n> **DISPOSITION: FILED, with the precedent cited so whoever closes it knows the shape is already proven.** It ranks ABOVE the `lenread` escape among the debts, because a second overlay strengthens the whole section while a short-read row adds one path.\n\n*The distinction worth carrying: an ORACLE gap (the rows cannot see a state they do reach) is closer to vacuity than a FIXTURE gap (the rows never reach the state at all). The first is sometimes L1. The second is coverage, and coverage is filed.*\n\n*** PASS-2, THE KIMI SUBSTITUTE SEAT: no break found, and THREE findings the other seats missed. The ask-again has now paid on every pass. ***\n\n**1. THE SIGNEDNESS SAFETY IS CONDITIONAL, and the condition is the same range invariant that proves termination.** `len > remain ? remain : len` compares a `size_t` against a signed `off_t`, so `remain` converts to unsigned -- **safe ONLY because `remain` is in `[1, BLOCK]`.** So the range proof is not merely about the loop terminating; **it is load-bearing for the comparison itself.** Had `remain` ever been <= 0 the conversion would produce an enormous unsigned value and the clamp would silently stop clamping. *Two independent consequences of one invariant, and only one of them was noticed by the other seats.*\n\n**2. A PROVABLY-EQUIVALENT EDIT THAT IS NOT COVERAGE DEBT:** `buf += lentoread` -> `buf += remain`. On every non-final iteration `len > remain`, so the two are equal; on the final iteration the loop exits before `buf` is read again. **Undetectable AND harmless** -- correctly classified as an equivalent mutant rather than an escape, which is a distinction this project has had to make before (`#449`'s guard-only widening measured EQUIVALENT).\n\n*** 3. A THIRD ESCAPE CLASS, AND IT IS ABOUT MY DETECTOR'S GEOMETRY RATHER THAN ITS ORACLE: *** a plausible 'optimization' that starts iteration k's overlay scan from iteration k-1's `overlay_nr` **passes every row I wrote** -- because my fixture pins exactly ONE geometry: a single overlay with only block 0 marked. With one overlay there is no layer ORDER to get wrong.\n\n> **That is a different kind of debt from the `lenread` escape.** That one is an oracle gap on an unexercised PATH (short reads). This is a FIXTURE gap -- the rows cannot distinguish layer-selection bugs because the fixture has only one layer to select. **A second overlay, with a different block marked, closes it** -- and the tree already knows this shape: `diff_diskimage_sync.c:570` says *'A SECOND OVERLAY IS WHAT MAKES THE ROW WORK, and it is not decoration.'*\n\n**FILED, not fixed** -- same reasoning as the `lenread` escape: not a floor breach, not a measured false pass against a shipped claim. **But it is the more valuable of the two to close**, because a second overlay would strengthen every row rather than adding one.\n\n*Fair criticism of my brief that I accept: the seat noted rows 3-5 were not inlined, so its answer to 'smallest wrong edit' had to reason from two rows rather than five. **A pass-2 brief should inline the DETECTOR, not just the product diff** -- the same inline-every-fact rule the carrier already states for packet-fed seats, applied to the artifact under review.*\n\n*** PASS-2 COVERAGE DEBT, FILED NOT FIXED (the stopping rule): a ONE-IDENTIFIER mutant escapes all five rows. ***\n\n**The mutant (deepseek, pass 2): `buf += lentoread;` -> `buf += lenread;`.**\n\n`lenread` is what `fread` actually RETURNED; `lentoread` is what was REQUESTED. **In every path the five rows exercise they are identical**, so the whole detector stays green. They diverge only on a SHORT READ, where `buf` would advance by less than `len` was decremented -- desynchronising the buffer from the offset for every later iteration.\n\n**WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT FIXED IN-ROUND, stated so nobody re-argues it:**\n  * it is **not a floor breach** -- but the ground I first gave was CIRCULAR and is corrected here. I wrote *'it nulls nothing in any path the rows reach'*, which **every escape satisfies by definition**, including `#462`'s M11 that was ruled a floor breach. *A wrong reason gets reused.* **The correct, checkable ground:** outside a short read `lenread == lentoread` is an IDENTITY, so the swap preserves the repair across the entire defect class this round closes; it diverges only in the short-read state, which the contract excludes -- and which is **not silent**, because the pre-existing `INCOMPLETE READ` `fatal()` announces it.\n  * it is **not a measured false pass** against a shipped claim, and not a wrong record. The stopping rule files everything else, and `#392` is what happens when it does not.\n  * it lands squarely on the **short-read path this round already declared out of scope and pre-existing** -- so it is the same debt seen from the mutant side rather than a new one.\n\n**What would close it:** a row that forces a short read in a NON-FINAL iteration -- an overlay data file truncated mid-range -- and asserts the bytes land at the right offsets afterwards. That row would also cover the pre-existing zero-fill concern, so **one row discharges both**.\n\n*Two seats, two different answers to the same question, and the sharper one won: another seat answered that the smallest passing edit is an INLINED equivalent of the same arithmetic -- true, but behaviourally identical, so it is readability debt rather than coverage debt. **The ask-again is worth the invocation precisely because seats differ on what counts as an escape.***\n\n*** #472 IN FLIGHT 2026-08-27: REPRODUCED, FIXED, AND MEASURED -- pass 2 running, nothing committed yet. ***\n\n**TEST-FIRST, on the committed build, before any edit:**\n```\n  ok    [LIVE] aligned read of block 0 is all overlay        = 512\n  ok    [LIVE] aligned read of block 1 is all base           = 512\n  FAIL  overlay unaligned: wrong-layer bytes in 1024 @ 511   = 512, want 1\n  ok    overlay unaligned: reports SUCCESS despite corruption = 1\n```\nExactly ONE real failure, both liveness controls green, on the PREDICTED numbers. The last row is the silent-corruption evidence: it returns success while handing back wrong bytes.\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE, and pass 1 is why the second row exists: ***\n```\n  arm                        wrong-layer row      never-wrote row\n  PRISTINE (committed)       FAIL 512, want 1     ok 0\n  MUTANT   (clamp only)      ok 1  <- ESCAPES     FAIL 511, want 0\n  FIXED    (clamp + stride)  ok 1                 ok 0\n```\n**Two seats independently named that mutant** -- clamp the length, keep the fixed stride. Under it the wrong-layer row reads 1, **which is the CORRECT answer**, because the buffer receives HOLES rather than wrong bytes. The rows are measurably non-redundant.\n\n*Measured in a COPIED tree, so no repo file was ever mutated and no sentinel was needed.*\n\n**THE FIX:** clamp `lentoread` to the current block's remainder; advance `buf` by what was consumed. Same shape as `diskimage_access__cue()` in the same file.\n\n**A seat supplied the algebra I had not derived:** `curofs + remain` **is** `(curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1`, identically -- so the clamp is *provably consistent* with the loop's existing advance rather than merely compatible with it.\n\n*** THREE THINGS CAUGHT ME, ALL BY GUARDS: ***\n  1. **`buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` appears TWICE.** The other is in `fwrite_helper`, where the fixed stride is CORRECT because that path refuses unaligned access. **Patching the first occurrence would have broken the write path**; the anchor-count guard stopped it.\n  2. **I reformatted 6 lines I never touched** -- a whitespace pass removed 7 trailing-tab lines when I had introduced 1. Reverted and re-applied; the diff is now 17+/3- with `-w` giving an identical stat.\n  3. **The mutant script's anchor went to 0 after the fix landed** and said so, rather than silently patching nothing.\n\n**Post-fix:** `52 rows, 0 failures, DISKIMAGE_IO_PASS`. All 11 offline differentials green. Twin trees and ALL THREE build trees byte-identical (an earlier draft said 'both' and was MEASURED FALSE -- it forgot the WSL arc tree, which was stale).\n\n**FILED, NOT FIXED:** the short-read path is unchanged -- on a short read in a non-final iteration `len` decrements by the requested amount while `totallenread` grows by the actual, so the caller's zero-fill can cover the wrong region. Pre-existing; the patch neither helps nor worsens it.\n\n*** CONFIRMED 2026-08-27 by an adversarial opus pass that tried to break all four steps and could not. Three refinements, and the first is a REAL NARROWING I had missed. ***\n\n**PRECONDITION: the defect requires `d->nr_of_overlays > 0`.** With no overlay the function takes a fast path (`:1347`) that is a single `fseek`+`fread` and is correct. My record did not state this. **It is not a get-out:** overlays come from the `V` prefix (`:1810`) and from `R:` (`:2095`), and `R:` is routine in this project's own rigs (`gate_ab.sh:82` uses `LUNA_IMG=\"R:...\"`, and `drive_guest.py` and several probes do the same).\n\n**UNALIGNED OFFSETS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE, with a census rather than an example:**\n  * `promemul/dec_prom.c:167-170` -> `:148` -- `lseek` stores an arbitrary 64-bit guest value as `current_file_offset`; `read()` passes it straight through with a guest length up to 64 MB. **Gives both faults.**\n  * `promemul/arcbios.c:1923` -> `:1761` -- ARC `Seek` stores an arbitrary 64-bit offset from guest memory; `Read` uses it with a length up to 64 MB. **Gives both faults.**\n  * `diskimage_scsicmd.c:725`/`:830` -- variable-length tape READ via `tape_offset`.\n  * `bootblock_iso9660.c:271-282` -- unaligned, but a fixed 256-byte length means one iteration, so **wrong-layer only**, no misalignment.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE IS AN ASYMMETRY I HAD NOT LOOKED FOR: THE WRITE SIDE HAS THE GUARD THE READ SIDE LACKS. *** `fwrite_helper` explicitly refuses a non-multiple length (`:1273-1280`) and an unaligned offset (`:1281-1286`), both returning 0, both under `#164`. `fread_helper` has **no such guard anywhere**. The read path was simply never given the check the write path was.\n\n**THE MECHANISM IS SHARPER THAN I STATED, in a way that matters for the detector:** iteration 1 is **positionally CORRECT** (`buf[k] = file[offset+k]`); its only fault is the LAYER. The positional corruption begins at iteration 2 and is caused by the fixed `buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` stride paired with a variable-start read. **So the two halves have different trigger conditions:** wrong-layer needs only an unaligned offset; duplication additionally needs `len > 512 - (offset & 511)`. A detector must cover both.\n\n**AND IT IS NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY ISSUE, which was checked explicitly:** `buf` advances 512 only when `lentoread == 512`, and any short `lentoread` zeroes `len` and ends the loop; `len` cannot underflow. **Silent wrong data, no overrun.**\n\n**The cue idiom is confirmed as the fix template** (`:1091-1144`): `chunk = CDROM_SECTOR_SIZE - intra; if (chunk > len) chunk = len;` with `buf + total_copied`. It clamps to bytes-remaining-in-sector AND indexes the buffer by bytes actually delivered -- **both halves of what the overlay loop lacks.**\n\n**ADJACENT, not claimed by this row:** on a short read the loop does `len -= lentoread` while `totallenread += lenread`, so a short read in a NON-FINAL iteration makes the caller's `memset(buf + lendone, ...)` zero the wrong region. Worth a row of its own if this is worked.\n\n*Honest limit the seat stated and I keep: no rig was measured actually issuing an unaligned >512-byte read against an overlaid disk. Reachability is READ, not MEASURED.*\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, GUEST-REACHABLE, AND IT CORRUPTS READ DATA. The most severe bare-title row worked so far. Verified by reading the loop; the mechanism is worse than the title says. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage.c:1358-1402`, the overlay read loop:\n\n```c\nfor (curofs=offset; len != 0;\n    curofs = (curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1) {\n        for (overlay_nr = d->nr_of_overlays-1; overlay_nr >= 0; overlay_nr --)\n                if (overlay_has_block(d, overlay_nr, curofs))   /* layer chosen for THIS block */\n                        break;\n\n        lentoread = len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE? OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE : len;   /* NOT clamped to the\n                                                       block's REMAINDER */\n        ... fread(buf, 1, lentoread, <chosen layer>) ...\n\n        len -= lentoread;\n        buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE;          /* fixed stride */\n}\n```\n\n**`lentoread` is clamped to the BLOCK SIZE, never to the bytes left in the CURRENT block.** So when `offset` is not block-aligned, the loop picks a layer for the block containing `curofs` and then reads up to a full block from it -- **running past that block's end into the next one or two blocks, which may live in a different layer.**\n\n**Worked example, offset 511, len 1024** (`OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` = 512):\n  * iteration 1: `curofs = 511` -> layer chosen for **block 0**; `lentoread = 512`; reads file offsets **511-1022** into `buf[0..511]`.\n  * `curofs = (511 | 511) + 1 = 512`; `buf += 512`.\n  * iteration 2: layer chosen for **block 1**; reads file offsets **512-1023** into `buf[512..1023]`.\n\n**The guest asked for offsets 511-1534 and receives: wrong-layer bytes in the first half, and file offsets 512-1022 DUPLICATED at two different buffer positions.** The tail it asked for (1023-1534) is never read at all. This is silent read corruption, not a refusal.\n\n*** THE LOOP'S OWN ADVANCE EXPRESSION PROVES UNALIGNED OFFSETS WERE ANTICIPATED, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS AN OVERSIGHT AND NOT AN UNDOCUMENTED PRECONDITION. *** `curofs = (curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1` rounds UP to the next block boundary. If `offset` were always block-aligned that expression would simply be `curofs + OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE`. **The author wrote alignment-handling into the advance and omitted it from the length.**\n\n**GUEST-REACHABLE** (seat's sequence, structurally consistent with the code, which carries no alignment guard anywhere): an overlaid ARC disk, guest writes blocks 0 and 2 so they land in different layers, then a byte-granular `Seek(511)` + `Read(1024)`. ARC exposes byte-granular seek/read, so the unaligned offset reaches this loop. **Not verified by execution -- needs a build.**\n\n**Observable for a detector:** the guest-visible read payload. Write distinct sentinel bytes into blocks that land in different layers, then read across the boundary from an unaligned offset; broken returns the wrong layer's sentinel and a duplicated range, fixed returns the requested bytes in order.\n\n*** THE FIX IDIOM IS ALREADY IN THIS FILE. *** `diskimage_access__cue()` (`:1083`) does exactly the right thing for the same shape of problem -- it clamps each step to the remainder of the current sector and writes at `buf + total_copied` rather than on a fixed stride (see its `memset(buf + total_copied, 0, chunk)` at the same offset discipline). **Transferred warrant: clamp `lentoread` to the current block's remainder, and advance `buf` by the amount actually consumed rather than by a constant.**\n\n**A SECOND, SEPARABLE HALF reported by the seat and NOT verified here:** a short `fread` on the flat (non-overlay) path is said to return zero even after reading bytes, causing the caller to zero the whole requested buffer. Recorded as the seat's claim; check it before it rides along with the fix, because a row covering two defects buries one when the other closes.\n\n\n\n*** THE SENTENCE ABOVE PREDICTED THAT ONE ROT-PACKET RUN 'reds `stale quiet` and discharges\nBOTH'.  THE RUN HAPPENED AND REFUTED THE SECOND HALF.  Corrected 2026-08-29. ***\n\n`regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/rot_20260829/` -- post-fix binary, one-line frozen-probe edit\n(packed format byte 0x20 -> 0x21):\n\n    base.log   GDROM_DEF_RESULT=60/60    stale quiet  0 vs 0      ok\n    rot.log    GDROM_DEF_RESULT=59/60    stale quiet  1 vs 0    **FAIL**\n\nThe two `GDROM_DEF_VECTOR` lines differ in EXACTLY ONE POSITION -- discriminating, not merely\ndisruptive, and proven by the probe's own vector rather than asserted.\n\n**`stale quiet` is DISCHARGED.  `stale how` is NOT**, and structurally cannot be by a rotted\npacket: the fix DECLINES rather than dying, so the session still exits cleanly.  Worse, it is\nthe WANTED-EQUALS-FAILURE shape -- the row wants `exit0` and a BROKEN `how` capture also\nyields `exit0` -- so only a measured red separates a working oracle from an inert one.  It\nneeds a mutant that exits AFTER the last budget check.  **Treat it as UNPROVEN until then.**\n\n*Found by a supplementary pass-2 seat applying the grep-for-siblings rule: the same predicted\nsentence sat in the probe comment AND here, and the measurement refuted both at once.*"
3070:     "note": "REAL — offset parsing still narrows through atoi and post-link failures leave a residual disk",
3158:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, host-side only -- AND IT IS HALF OF A FAMILY, which neither seat could see from its own row. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage.c:1722` reads `override_base_offset = atoi(fname);`. **`atoi` returns `int`**, while the field is 64-bit -- `:1729-1730` prints it with `PRIi64`. A 64-bit-capable option is parsed by a 32-bit function.\n\nThe `< 0` check at `:1728` catches a result that lands negative, but **not a truncation that wraps to a plausible positive value**, which is the silent case.\n\n*** THE FAMILY: THIS IS A MISSED SITE FROM `#414`. *** `:1681` says *\"#414: strtoll, not atoi, and BOTH fields\"* -- that round converted the GEOMETRY fields at `:1693`/`:1698` and left the base offset on `atoi`. See [[b122]], the same round's OTHER residual (converted, but the endptr discarded). **Two rows, one incomplete conversion.**\n\n**NOT guest-reachable**: `diskimage_add()` has exactly two callers, `main.c:707` (`-d`) and `emul_parse.c:558` (config), both at startup. Severity is host-input robustness.\n\n"
3394:     "note": "VERDICT: **`return 0` IS REFUTED -- not merely a sketch, but a MEASURED-BY-READING\nBEHAVIOURAL REGRESSION on two controllers with ZERO benefit on the third.  The right shape\nis `diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp, d, 0x05, 0x20, 0x00)` PLUS the fall-through\n`return 1` -- the file's own refusal idiom at four shipped sites.**  Every claim below was\nre-verified by me against the source before recording.\n\n*** MY CENSUS WAS CONTAMINATED AND SO IS THE ROW'S.  Both counted the COMMENT HEADER and the\nDEFINITION as calls. ***\n\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message'          -> 25   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message(xferp'    -> 23   CALLS\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition'                     ->  5   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp'               ->  3   CALLS\n    grep -c $'^\\tcase '                                             -> 22   top-level labels\n\nSo **the row's \"the only one of 25\" is wrong under ANY unit** -- 25 is the contaminated raw\ncount.  The correct statement is **\"the only one of 20 TOP-LEVEL ARMS\"** (22 labels forming\n19 bodies, since MODE_SENSE/10, READ/READ_10 and WRITE/WRITE_10 share bodies, plus the\ndefault).  My own `~30` counted NESTED labels.  *A re-deriver caution worth keeping: \n`grep -E '^\\tcase'` silently matches NOTHING, because `\\t` is a literal `t` in ERE -- use\n`$'\\t'`.  I hit that exact trap on this file earlier today.*\n\n**THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM SURVIVES, verified arm by arm**: all 19 bodies call the status helper\non every completing path.  ONE QUALIFIER: at PATH level the two `return 2` first passes also\nset no status -- correctly, because the command is incomplete and the DATA_OUT re-entry sets\nit, and all three SCSI controllers re-call.  My return enumeration was exact.\n\n*** WHY `return 0` IS WORSE THAN A SKETCH -- three consumers, each read: ***\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:500-505`** -- `res == 0` sets **`d->scripts_running = 0`**.  A guest\n    probing one unsupported opcode **kills its own SCRIPTS engine**; no status phase ever.\n  * **`dev_asc.c:1395-1400`** -- `ok == 0` gives `STATE_DISCONNECTED` + `NCRINTR_DIS`.  The\n    guest sees a target DISCONNECT instead of CHECK CONDITION: retry/reset instead of\n    REQUEST SENSE.  **This also refutes the row's own state_note, which says asc does not\n    test for 0 at all** -- its select path does.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c:1528-1541`** -- the `res == 0` arm sets **no error bit**, and `status_byte()`\n    raises `WDCS_ERR` only from `d->error`.  So on wdc, `return 0` is guest-INDISTINGUISHABLE\n    from success-with-no-data.\n\n> **`return 0` helps no consumer and harms two.**  That is a stronger refutation than my\n> structural one, and it is the form a future round should cite.\n\n**THE FIX, WITH ITS WARRANT:** CHECK-CONDITION-then-`return 1` is ALREADY this file's refusal\nidiom at four shipped sites, and all four controllers demonstrably handle it today.  `d` is\nnon-NULL in the default arm (the NULL path returned earlier).  #417's latch semantics come\nfree: REQUEST SENSE reads `d->sense_key/asc/ascq` and the next success retires them.\n*The state_note's cited precedent is the WRONG one -- that shape exists only because `d` is\nNULL there and `check_condition` would dereference it; its own comments say `// ?` and `TODO`.*\n\n**MUST NOT BREAK:** (a) do NOT allocate `data_in` -- all three SCSI controllers pick the\nDATA_IN phase off `data_in != NULL`, and status-only completion is already TEST_UNIT_READY's\nshape; (b) leave the pre-switch not-connected `return 0` alone; (c) `est/` byte-identical\npropagation, shared file; (d) **the fix ALSO cures `mb89352halt`'s mechanism** -- `status_len`\nbecomes 1, so the PH_STAT read no longer trips 'read longer than buffer?' into\n`cpu->running = 0`, consistent with the standing ONE-ROUND-for-both ruling.\n\n*** THE ATAPI ROUTE IS REAL AND IS THE **WRONG WITNESS CHANNEL**, which inverts what I\nassumed when I raised it. ***  `dev_wdc.c:1515` does feed packet commands in, and `res == 1`\nwith no data gives PHASE_COMPLETED and no error bit.  **But `atapi_st->status` is read\nNOWHERE in dev_wdc.c** -- so an ATAPI witness stays RED after a dispatcher-only fix, because\nit witnesses the COMBINED dispatcher+wdc defect.  The discriminating channels for THIS fix\nare **asc** (ICCS fifo byte 0x00 -> 0x02, pmax rig) or **mb89352** (loudest pre-fix symptom\non record: the emulated CPU stops).  The wdc consumer repair is its own hunk in a different\nfile and should be filed separately -- the one-omission-in-one-shared-function warrant does\nnot stretch to it.\n\nRANKING: raises reachability BREADTH within the band, not harm class.  Keep rank 3.  Plausible\nbut UNMEASURED: MMC opcodes 0x46 (GET CONFIGURATION) and 0x4A (GET EVENT STATUS NOTIFICATION)\nare not among the 19 arms and period ATAPI attach code issues them routinely -- one flagged\nboot would settle it.\n\n**FILED, NOT FIXED (a sibling this round does not touch):** `SPACE`'s NESTED `default:`\n`fatal()`s and then FALLS THROUGH to the status helper, **telling the guest GOOD for an\nunimplemented SPACE type**.  Same false-success class, inside a KNOWN arm rather than the\ndefault one, so b120u's fix does not reach it.  b120r-shaped."
3398:   "witness": "none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3/4, but building the probe needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids. First in line with the other build-blocked rows.",
3611:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: the mechanism is real but ALREADY DEFENDED IN TREE, on two levels. The seat's closing line is *'Cost of a round is zero. The detector already exists.'* ***\n\n**Guest sequence traced:** `str #0` to `0x42000000+0x300` (`TIMER_1_LOAD`), which always calls `reload_timer_value`.\n\n*** THE OBSERVABLE IS THE INTERESTING PART, AND IT IS WHY THIS ROW CANNOT HAVE AN ORDINARY PROBE: the failure is host SIGALRM catch-up RETURNING versus NEVER RETURNING. **A guest MMIO value cannot distinguish the two.** *** That is a case where the defect is real and the guest channel is genuinely absent -- worth keeping as an example, because the usual assumption is that a real defect implies a guest-visible oracle.\n\n**TWO in-tree defences of the same class, both named:**\n  * **device level** -- `footbridge_effective_cycles()`: a never-zero span, register left raw, 8254-style `0 -> 2^width`, cited in the helper's own comment;\n  * **core level** -- `timer_clamp_freq` plus `TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP` (`timer.c:97-109`, `:281-290`).\n\n**DISPOSITION: verify the two defences cover the traced sequence, then CLOSE.** Not verified here. If they do, this belongs with the already-done rows rather than the open queue.\n\n"
3736:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE TITLE'S PREMISE IS MIS-STATED AND THE UNDERLYING DEFECT IS REAL AND WORSE THAN EITHER THE TITLE OR THE SEAT DESCRIBED. It is a MISSING `else`. ***\n\nThe title blames *wrap-around*. A farmed seat checked that premise -- as the brief asked -- and refuted it: ordinary periodic wrap is handled. **But it confirmed the mechanism, and reading the code shows a sharper defect than the one it named.**\n\n`src/cpus/cpu_mips_coproc.c:796-819`:\n\n```c\nint32_t compare_diff = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COMPARE];\ndouble hz;\n\nif (compare_diff < 0)\n        hz = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COUNT];        /* (A) */\n\nif (compare_diff == 0)                         /* SEPARATE statement */\n        hz = 0;                                /* (B) */\nelse\n        hz = (double)emulated_hz / (double)compare_diff;   /* (C) */\n```\n\n**(A) IS AN UNCONDITIONALLY DEAD STORE.** The `if/else` at (B)/(C) is a separate statement, and both its arms assign `hz` -- so whatever (A) wrote is always overwritten. When `compare_diff < 0`, (C) runs and yields a **NEGATIVE** `hz`; `if (hz > 0)` at `:812` is then false and **the timer is neither created nor updated**.\n\n**The three cases, exhaustively:**\n  * `compare_diff > 0` -- (C) gives a positive rate; timer set. **The only working path.**\n  * `compare_diff == 0` -- (B) gives 0; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n  * `compare_diff < 0` -- (A) computed the intended value and (C) throws it away for a negative one; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n\n**Consequence, which is what the title was reaching for:** if the timer was never created it stays NULL, so **no timer interrupt is ever delivered again**; if it already existed it keeps its stale rate. Either way a guest write that ought to reprogram the timer silently does nothing.\n\n*** THE DEAD STORE IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING, because it shows INTENT. *** The author wrote handling for `compare_diff < 0` and it has never executed. This is not a design choice being second-guessed -- it is a chain of `if`s that lost its `else`, and (A) is the evidence.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- PARTLY SETTLED, and the unsettled half is recorded as unsettled rather than asserted.** The gate is `emulated_hz > 0` at `:797`; **21 assignments to `emulated_hz` exist across the tree.**\n  * **arc: DORMANT, verified here** -- `machine_arc.c` contains ZERO `emulated_hz` assignments, so the gate cannot pass.\n  * **pmax: OPEN.** It DOES set `emulated_hz` (`machine_pmax.c:96`, `:160`), so the `:797` gate passes. The seat argued pmax is nonetheless dormant because its default R3000 is `EXC3K`, citing `cpu_dyntrans.c` -- **a file not read here, so this is recorded as the seat's claim, not as verified.** Settle it before ranking this row.\n  * the seat named `algor` (RM5200/EXC4K, `machine_algor.c:49`) as directly reachable. Also unverified here.\n\n**Observable for a detector:** guest-visible timer interrupt delivery -- `Cause.IP7`. Broken: no assertion at all after a write with `compare_diff <= 0`, or delivery stuck at the stale cadence. Fixed: the newly programmed cadence.\n\n**Related:** see `dfreq`, whose closure text also concerns the MIPS compare write converting a distance-to-deadline into a frequency. **Check for overlap before opening a round on either** -- a queue id covering two defects is how one gets buried when the other closes.\n\n",
3964:     "note": "CLOSED 2026-08-29.  **No new decision was taken here.**  The row already carried assess 9/9 with the full roster NAMED (agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax, opus5), an explicit CLOSE verdict, and a fable5 adjudication of the same date recording it as CLOSURE-READY and NOT CLOSED.  Flipping the state is the only thing that was owed.  *** IT SAT IN `held` WITH AN ANSWERED QUESTION, which is the state-vs-substance mismatch this project has recorded before: a future reader who trusts states reads `held` as 'waiting', and that is false. ***  It is the unread-review shape one level up -- the seat work was done, delivered and never banked.  Found while triaging the 75 held rows for the owner: only THREE of them were closure-ready, which itself corrected a claim I had just made that 43% of the backlog was seat-blocked (only 2 of 75 carry a seat marker)."
4005:     "note": "PACKET, AND WRONG -- recorded because being wrong in the author's own direction is the finding. Voted DROP as a 'likely duplicate of shipped SCSI length fixes', which is exactly what the brief flagged as its prime suspicion. A file-blind seat cannot check a duplicate claim, and agreed with the prompt instead. Its blindness is still a feature elsewhere; it is a liability precisely here, on repo fact.  [panel_20260817_174701]"
4053:     "note": "*** MEASURED ACROSS THREE GUESTS AND FIVE INSTRUMENTED ARMS. The lie is real, it REACHES the guest, the guest DOES read the field -- and on the shipping value no guest on any rig is harmed. Priority drops; the fix does not become unnecessary. ***\n* TRAFFIC: pmax/OpenBSD 2.2 (old-style rz driver) issues ZERO MODE SENSE across a full multiuser boot plus disklabel, mount and raw dd -- a clean negative. arc/OpenBSD 2.2 issues 5x MODE SENSE(6) page 4: asked 32, got 32, TOLD 132, true 36. That reproduces the brief's capture exactly. luna88k/OpenBSD 7.7 issues 11x across pages 0/4/8: asked 254, got 254, TOLD 98.\n* *** THE WRAP IS ALREADY EXERCISED IN NORMAL OPERATION: luna88k asks 254, so 254+100=354 WRAPS to 98. *** It merely lands on a value the guest tolerates. Measured sweep: reported==0 at exactly alloc==156, and reported < true-1 for alloc in [156,190]. No rig asks 156, so the defect is ONE allocation-length change away from the arm-C behaviour below, not a hypothetical.\n* THE CHANNEL IS LIVE, proved by a POSITIVE CONTROL rather than assumed: sending cyl=1234/heads=9 changes arc's attach line from '2081 cyl, 16 head, 63 sec' to '1234 cyl, 9 head, 188 sec'. The response body IS consumed.\n* AND IT REFUTED ITS OWN DRAFT: it had written that a page-4 rpm change proves consumption; arm D showed disklabel still printed 3600 because that value comes from the ON-DISK LABEL, not from MODE SENSE. The control killed its own claim before it shipped.\n* HARM, MEASURED: luna88k told-98 vs told-35/11 produce ORDERED TRANSCRIPTS IDENTICAL, 0 diff lines across 97 lines each. But told-0 FLIPS CONTROL FLOW -- 32 MODE SENSE instead of 11, including MODE SENSE(10) fallbacks and page 5. OpenBSD 7.7 branches on this byte. So 98 and 132 sit in a tolerated 'nonzero and >= true' region; 0 does not.\n* *** THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG: `retlen -= 100` writes the ALLOCATION LENGTH. *** The spec wants true_len-1, measured as 11/23/35/35/43 for pages 0/1/3/4/5 -- the allocation length is correct only by coincidence at alloc==191. The true value is not known at :607 because the page is written later at :630+, so the write must MOVE AFTER the pagecode switch.\n* BLAST RADIUS: nothing reads retlen after :607 (next use :746 is an assignment in the READ case), and data_in[0] is read NOWHERE outside diskimage_scsicmd.c across all of src/ -- every HBA sizes from data_in_len, so the guest is the only consumer. No overflow: max write is q+29 = 45 against a >=100-byte buffer.\n* DETECTOR built and feasible offline. TWO DESIGN POINTS THAT MATTER: the rows MUST SPAN PAGES {0,1,3,4,5}, because every page-4 case has true_len 36 and a mutant hardcoding 35 would pass a page-4-only test; and an INVARIANCE row -- the reported length must not vary with the allocation length -- NAMES THE DEFECT RATHER THAN A VALUE, so a '+100 -> +0' half-fix cannot pass it. -ffunction-sections/-Wl,--gc-sections is required or mymkstemp/debug_indentation fail to link.\n* ADJACENT, measured en route: MODE SENSE(10) writes the 6-BYTE header layout, so a conformant guest parses block_desc_len=0 while 8 real descriptor bytes sit at offset 8 -- STRICTLY WORSE than b120r, and unreached today only because no rig issues MS10. And data_in_len is set to the FULL allocation length even when far less data exists (alloc 255 -> 255 bytes, 219 of them zeros) where a real drive transfers 36, which affects residual logic.\n* HONEST UNKNOWN, stated by the seat: three guests measured, ALL OpenBSD. Whether a NetBSD/Ultrix/Linux driver asks an allocation length in [156,190] was not measured.\n* It also caught a citation error of mine: REQUEST SENSE forcing is :964-967, not the :957-960 my brief claimed. Verified and corrected above.  [agent b120r 2026-08-19]"
4156:     "note": "PASS 1 MEASURE SEAT -- built a private tree, BOOTED THE RIG, and refuted three of my own measurements.\n* REACHABILITY, MEASURED POSITIVE, so this is a FIX not a document: instrumented the SCSI dispatch and booted luna88k UNMODIFIED to login:. The guest issues SYNCHRONIZE CACHE (0x35) ONCE, immediately after the log line '/dev/sd0a: MARKING FILE SYSTEM CLEAN' -- the exact moment the command exists for, and the worst moment to lose a write tail. pmax runs OpenBSD 2.2 whose scsi/ contains no 0x35 at all; landisk is ATA, not SCSI.\n* THE DETECTOR IT BUILT AND THEN ATTACKED: 34 rows, 17 fail on shipping code, 0 with the fix, identical at -O0 and -O2, 0 faults. THREE OF ITS FOUR ATTACKS DEFEATED IT: nblk 4->16 (ONE DIGIT -- one whole buffer flushes completely), exit() instead of _exit(), and the one my brief did not list -- ONE MORE SCSI READ AFTER THE SYNC, whose my_fseek flushes the pending write. Any realistic-looking test that does anything at all after the sync is silently green.\n* THE VACUITY CLASS THAT WOULD HAVE SUNK THE ROUND: run the same binary with its workdir on /mnt/c (drvfs, st_blksize=512 -- where _scratchpad, _images and BOTH RIGS live) and EVERY byte row passes with the defect fully present, because each 512-byte fwrite reaches the kernel immediately. Its [VACUITY CONTROL] row fires and declares the section meaningless. The --wrap=fsync/fflush call-recording rows stay red on drvfs -- filesystem-independent, and the ONLY rows that can see defect (b) at all.\n* Q1 NO do_fsync GATE, with costs: dirty fflush+fsync 5.85 ms ext4, clean 0.56 ms. The flood surface ALREADY SHIPS (the handler fsyncs unconditionally today); the fix makes it at most ~3x, synchronous and single-threaded, so a guest cannot get asynchronous amplification. Not the cflood shape -- there the cost is a diagnostic, here the cost IS the semantics.\n* Q2 YES, same class as #286, and #416/#417 already settled the harder half: :907-910 already returns MEDIUM ERROR for HOST-side failures, and #417 tested it against a real errno (RLIMIT_FSIZE -> EFBIG). Honest UNKNOWN: Linux >=4.13 reports an fsync failure ONCE and drops the dirty pages, so a LATER sync returns success after data was already lost.\n* Q3 DO NOT sync d->f under overlays, and it is worse than pointless: diskimage.c:1984 opens d->f in mode r whenever prefix_R is set while :1971 sets writable=1, so R: -- WHAT BOTH RIGS USE -- gives a writable disk whose base handle is READ-ONLY, and the temp overlay is already unlinked. fflush on a read-mode stream is UB in C99 7.21.5.2p2; fsync on O_RDONLY is rc=0 on glibc but UNKNOWN on NetBSD/OpenBSD/Solaris, where it would turn every R:/CD-ROM sync into a spurious MEDIUM ERROR. Plus 14.34 ms to fsync a 512 MB untouched file on drvfs.\n* Q5 ONE COMMAND: luna88k issued START STOP UNIT 5x, ALL with cmd[4]=0x01 (START, no LOEJ, no STOP). The durability-adjacent case was never observed.\n* ADVERSARIAL -- THREE DEFECTS IN MY PROPOSED FUNCTION, one measured into silent corruption: (i) int ok = 1 returns SUCCESS for a disk with nothing open, losing the #134 diagnostic; (ii) THE ORDERING HOLE -- it sets ok=0 on a failed overlay-data fsync AND THEN FSYNCS THE BITMAP ANYWAY, publishing 'the overlay owns block N' while N's bytes are not durable. It BUILT that state and drove the shipping read path: the guest reads 0x00 from the overlay's HOLE and the base's good 0xBB bytes are GONE -- silent corruption of data the guest never touched, reported as a successful read. It does NOT fall through to the base. Honest counterweight: NOT reproducible in the current write path (BIT_SET_BUT_DATA_MISSING=0 across n=1..129; the per-block fseeks keep them in lockstep), so it is a hole the FIX would INTRODUCE, not a live defect; (iii) directory-entry durability CHECKED AND NOT A HOLE -- user overlays open r+ and must pre-exist, and the only created file is the unlinked temp overlay. An honest negative.\n* Also found: there is NO fclose of d->f or the overlays at shutdown anywhere. A clean exit() flushes stdio so a normal quit is safe, but a SIGKILLed emulator loses the tail regardless.\n* ITS OWN ERROR, reported rather than dropped: its first detector left c.nblk UNINITIALIZED, so every overlay row went red -- AGREEING WITH ITS HYPOTHESIS -- and at -O2 malloc(512*garbage) SIGSEGVd into 4 FAULTS it nearly reported as an overlay-path defect. Its own words: a red row that confirms your hypothesis is the dangerous kind.  [panel_20260817_215052]"
4164:     "note": "*** NAMED A MEASUREMENT NOBODY HAD RUN AND TOLD ME TO RUN IT BEFORE WRITING THE CHANGELOG SENTENCE: an fflush-ONLY (no fsync) arm. Its words: 'If that arm persists after _exit, the kill-and-check row is not an fsync test.' I RAN IT AND IT WAS RIGHT. *** MEASURED, one 512-byte write: fflush only -> 512 bytes DURABLE to a separate reader; fsync only (what ships today) -> 0. So _exit() simulates PROCESS death, not host death -- the page cache belongs to the KERNEL and outlives the process -- and a kill-and-check test therefore measures the missing FFLUSH, never the missing FSYNC. This SPLITS THE DEFECT: (a) missing fflush is byte-testable and bounded; (b) overlays never fsynced at all is NOT byte-testable by ANY process-kill test, needs the --wrap rows, and is unbounded on real power loss. It also placed the detector correctly (beside diff_diskimage_io.c, NOT on the #411 write-fail fixtures) and filed six things explicitly NOT to do this round.  [panel_20260817_215052]"
4220:     "note": "PASS 2 ON THE DIFF, AND IT KILLED MY DETECTOR. Verdict SHIP-WITH-CHANGES.\n* *** THE DETECTOR WAS STILL VACUOUS: --wrap counted HOW MANY calls and never WHICH FILE, so FOUR mutants that restore real data loss all passed green *** -- v1 rename f_bitmap to f_data (bitmap NEVER synced), v2 invert the data/bitmap order (the rule the code itself calls load-bearing), v3 delete the ordering continue, v4 sync d->f instead of the bitmap. Every one keeps the totals at 2 and 2. Smallest defeating edit: v1, ONE identifier. And the row NAMES overclaimed -- 'fflushes data and bitmap' passed with the bitmap untouched, and the gate grepped those exact names as its evidence.\n* *** AND THE FIXTURE HAD NEVER WRITTEN A BYTE. *** SCSICMD_WRITE is 0x0a = WRITE(6) driven with a 10-byte CDB, so the length parsed as 256 blocks; and the handler requires data_out_offset == size, which the fixture never set. Both overlay files were 0 bytes after every run -- corroborated by identical 0-byte leftovers already in the repo from an earlier run. THAT is why the file-blind mutants lived: with nothing written there is no durability property to check, only call counts.\n* Q1 THE `continue` IS RIGHT -- it tried and could not build a state where skipping is worse; a stale bitmap makes a read fall through to the base (stale but real) rather than to the overlay's hole (zeros). TWO CORRECTIONS AROUND IT: the comment overclaims, because after 4 writes with NO sync an independent reader already sees bitmap[0]=0x07 -- the per-block fseeks already pushed both to the page cache, so the continue declines to FORCE publication rather than preventing it; and a multi-overlay disk reports CHECK CONDITION when a non-last overlay's fsync fails even though every guest byte is durable.\n* ADVERSARIAL: THE DURABILITY CLAIM IS FALSE UNDER R:. diskimage_add_overlay(d, tmpname, true) unlinks BOTH overlay files right after opening (:177-184; measured fstat().st_nlink == 0), so they are anonymous temporaries no post-crash reader can open. Not introduced by the diff -- R: is meant to be ephemeral -- but the new comment presents R: as the motivating case, which it should not.\n* Q2 safe for luna88k (d == NULL returns before the switch, so CHECK CONDITION needs #134's NULL d->f or a real error). But it MEASURED an exposure the diff introduced: for r:/CD-ROM the new FLAT branch fflushes and fsyncs a handle opened \"r\" -- precisely what the function's own comment says it avoids. The comment reasons its way to the right rule for the overlay branch and then does not apply it to the branch it wrote. FIXED in this round.\n* Q5 FOUR COMMENTS MEASURED FALSE, two of them mine from pass 1: the gate's 'drop the --wrap flags and the rows still pass against nothing' (they FAIL CLOSED -- 7 rows/4 failed without both, 2 failed without --wrap=fflush); the selfmutant comment's -fno-optimize-sibling-calls analogy (omitting the flags yields SELFMUTANT_SETUP, which selfmutant.py's own header says is NEVER scored as a detection, so it taught the wrong hazard class); the diskimage.c comment attributing the fflush-first note to fwrite_helper when it lives in overlay_set_block_in_use at :1207-1211; and the fixture's 'Issue a WRITE(10)'.\n* Residual filed, not fixed: IDE FLUSH CACHE has no durability path at all (grep -i flush dev_wdc.c is empty).  [panel_20260818_095322]"
4276:     "note": "*** REACHED THE PASS-2 HEADLINE INDEPENDENTLY, from reading alone: 'Overlay rows' names overclaim. They count calls, not targets' and 'DO NOT SHIP THE DETECTOR AS EVIDENCE OF (b) UNTIL THE WRAP NAMES THE FD.' *** The measure seat proved the same thing with four mutants; grok saw it in the source. It also prescribed the exact read-only fix that was adopted -- 'if nr_of_overlays==0 && !d->writable, return 1 without touching the stream' -- and found TWO comment defects nobody else did: the new comment said diskimage_scsicmd.cc when the file is .c, and MY PATCH HAD INSERTED diskimage_sync() BETWEEN diskimage_dump_info()'s DOC COMMENT AND ITS BODY, orphaning that comment above an unrelated function -- my anchor was the signature, and a signature is not the top of a function when a doc block precedes it. Both fixed. Siblings it filed rather than chased: START STOP UNIT, SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) 0x91, WDCC_FLUSHCACHE.  [panel_20260818_095322]"
4284:     "note": "FABLE REGRESS ADJUDICATION of verdict_20260819_135703 at 6014b7e. Badge `s` disclosed: same-model adjudication of the main loop's records, not a two-family check.\n* *** Q2 REFUTED MY OWN SUSPICION USING THE BATTERY'S OWN LOG. *** I wrote that no gate asserts the guest-visible CHECK CONDITION. Lines 554 and 559 say otherwise (verified here): `overlay: a failed sync reports CHECK CONDITION 2` and `nothing open: sync reports CHECK CONDITION, not GOOD 2` -- FAULT-INJECTED via fail_fsync_fd returning EIO, driven through the real diskimage_scsicommand() with a WRITE(10)-then-SYNCHRONIZE-CACHE CDB pair, not a direct call. So gate 2 EXERCISED the changed path discriminatingly rather than merely not breaking on it. The live gates ran the success leg for real: luna88k reached login in gates 5 and 7.\n* Q1 MY FRAMING WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. Too strong: the battery is NOT the evidence the harness commits did not break the emulator -- GIT IS. `git diff --name-only 1ba2cd4..6014b7e` minus regress/tools/docs leaves exactly the three #437 files, so no build input outside #437 changed and the harness commits COULD NOT have altered the binary. Too weak: the 16/16 IS evidence about those commits -- it is the first end-to-end execution of their product, 16/16 with 0 skipped means no gate silently dropped out, and the selfmutant controls DETECTED in this run.\n* AND A FRAMING ERROR I REPEATED TWICE TODAY: diskimage_sync() does NOT sit on a path 'every rig's disk traffic crosses'. ONLY command 0x35 reaches it; ordinary READ/WRITE cross diskimage_scsicommand() but not the sync handler. The blast radius is narrower than I said.\n* #437 touched THREE src/ files, not one: diskimage.c, diskimage_scsicmd.c, diskimage.h. 'One src/ COMMIT' was true; the round record should name the three files.\n* Q3 NO EMERGENCY RE-RUN for 20ce22e and 7e3b120 -- every changed line is gate 2's surface or non-battery tooling, and the binary is bit-identical by construction, so a full battery would re-measure 14 unchanged gates against an unchanged binary: ritual, not evidence. ONE CHEAP CONDITION: selftest_mutation.sh's own header says run it after any change to gate 2, and that rule was triggered. Run gate 3 once at HEAD.\n* Q4 VACUITY, NAMED NOT SURVEYED: (1) gate_asan_sweep is a CONSTRUCTION sweep -- 37 machine types construct under ASan and nothing more, so *** #437's NEW CODE HAS NEVER RUN UNDER ASan ***; (2) gate_hygiene's pattern set contains ZERO occurrences of sync/sense/sd0, so the new guest-visible error class is invisible to it; (3) gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT -- not hypothetical, the measured LDM/STM defect was live at this HEAD; (4) selftest_mutation's green means ONE differential of eleven can fail, and its apparatus carries smhang/smnotland unfixed; (5) gate_ab is coarse rather than vacuous -- marker parity 1:1:1 accepted ~0.44% instruction drift. CHECKED CLEAN: gate_upstream's empty-both-sides hole is closed, gate 5 carries mutant-derived NINSTRS floors, and the old prompt-predicate hole in arm_flags_probe.py is CLOSED (its own 7-day-old memory note claiming otherwise is stale).\n* Q5 QUEUE RISK THE BATTERY CANNOT SEE, ranked: armbdt (the only MEASURED defect, battery structurally green over it); idesync (the #437 CLASS on the other disk stack, and the ATA rig boots a ramdisk kernel that issues no flush traffic); b120r; the four wdc residuals including a guest-set malloc count behind CHECK_ALLOCATION; the three selfmutant apparatus items; and m437multi carries INVERTED risk -- the battery now PINS the conservative behaviour that item wants changed, so its fix round must expect a SCHEDULED RED, not a regression."
4324:     "note": "*** VERDICT: REAL, BUT DO NOT CLOSE AS WORDED. `4d0981d` completely fixes SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) / 0x35; SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) / 0x91 still returns guest-visible GOOD without flushing anything. *** All three phases answered separately, which is what this row's three held stages needed: ASSESS real, RESEARCH go-with-changes, REVIEW no-go for closure. VERIFIED BEFORE RECORDING: `diskimage_scsicmd.c` defines and handles only `SCSICMD_SYNCHRONIZE_CACHE` (0x35) -- there is no 0x91 case -- and `dev_asc.c:1284-1287` reads `if (d->xferp != NULL && d->xferp->status != NULL) write(status[0]); else write(0x00);`, so ABSENT STATUS BECOMES GOOD. A valid 16-byte 0x91 CDB passes the group-length check, falls to the unimplemented default, and the guest is told GOOD while stdio-buffered overlay and bitmap data can still be unflushed. IT ALSO CORRECTED THE ORIGINAL FILING'S PREMISE: the filing assumed the BASE file was already flushed (#134). It was not -- before `4d0981d` the handler called `fsync()` with no `fflush()`, so even the flat base's stdio buffer was unflushed. **The shipped commit is therefore BETTER than its own filing claimed**, repairing both defects. AND IT CLOSED THE 0x35 QUESTION PROPERLY rather than asserting it: no ordinary 0x35 path returns GOOD with dirty data pending -- a missing data handle fails; a missing bitmap handle is unreachable because `diskimage_add_overlay()` appends only after opening BOTH files; a read-only flat image cannot hold guest-written dirty data. DISPOSITION IT PROPOSES, and it is the honest one: either route 0x91 through the same helper, or explicitly NARROW `ovsync` to SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) and TRANSFER the 0x91 instance to `b120` -- after which `ovsync` may close. It also checked and found **no dedicated SC(16) row exists**, and that the pass-2 statement claiming the 0x91 sibling was filed is NOT explicit in `b120`'s record. That is a wrong record to fix, not a filing to assume. Coverage gap named too: the committed differential exercises only 0x35, and the later coverage commit gates ordering without adding a 0x91 case."
4451:    "note": "Measured during R3's review, and recorded so the record does not read as fuller coverage than exists. The straddle does NOT catch: (1) a mutant that scales BOTH the print and the comparison - only the literal config-pin row catches that, which is why both row kinds stay; (2) an in-body change to STALL or BACKSTOP, because nothing straddles either and the under-leg accepts BACKSTOP as a stated reason; (3) `>` changed to `>=` at the comparison, which scores zero red rows. An in-body budget change is caught only to within one instruction. Closing (2) needs a stall/backstop straddle of the same shape.  closure.by normalised 2026-08-25 from 'opus' to 'opus5' -- the ledger's declared roster has opus5 and no 'opus'; a one-row spelling variant that every non-empty check passes."
4495:     "note": "HARNESS-INTEGRITY CLUSTER TRIAGE: eight rows about the instruments themselves, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE PANEL VOTED 5-0 TO DROP `constblind` AND THE VOTE WAS WRONG -- CHECKED, NOT TAKEN. ***  agy, grok, glm, deepseek and minimax all said drop it as 'already audited and fixed'; minimax even named the closing commit (cdc4849). Every one of them was reading the SAME SENTENCE -- the row's own hold, 'AUDITED: diff_timer was the only one exposed; now fixed'. That audit covered SEVEN differentials. THERE ARE THIRTEEN NOW. Six arrived after it, and re-running the audit's own test against today's tree found a LIVE INSTANCE: diff_rtc_range.c, written HOURS EARLIER IN THIS SESSION BY ME, reads TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY on the expected side of three rows.\n\nMEASURED, and it is the constblind shape in one screen: changing TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY to 1073741823.0 left all three clamp rows GREEN -- they printed the new value and passed -- while only an absolute-consequence row noticed. Fixed in the same commit by asserting `TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY == (double) INT_MAX`, derived from OUTSIDE the header, exactly as diff_timer.c:276-280 already does. Gate 2 PASS at 259 checks.\n\n*** THE LESSON IS BIGGER THAN THE ROW: A UNANIMOUS VERDICT DERIVED FROM ONE STALE RECORD IS ONE STALE RECORD, NOT FIVE CONFIRMATIONS. ***  Five seats agreeing looks like strong evidence and was worth exactly as much as the sentence they all read. The packet-fed seats COULD NOT have checked it; the two file-reading seats COULD have and did not. This is the clearest case yet for the standing rule that anything checkable in under two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged -- and the check here was one grep.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- four-way split, recorded not voted: gateflr (agy, minimax), pcheck (grok), optrow (glm, deepseek: 'the only measured false pass with a known one-flag fix').\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 10.3 KB, grok 10.0 KB, kimi 25.4 KB, glm 13.5 KB, deepseek 7.7 KB, minimax). Codex RATE-LIMITED, seventh panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEPT, with grok suggesting it close as DOCUMENT rather than stay held. The budget straddle cannot see three mutant shapes that are NAMED but not implemented -- so the honest question is whether naming them without building them is a finding or a to-do list.",
4522:     "note": "WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.\n\nITS VERDICT: VERDICT: Do `gateflr` first; DROP `census`, `constblind` as an open finding, and `strad` as currently filed.\n\nCAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel's own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a '+1' mutant that 'admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green' -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: 'do tfreq first' (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 'CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store'), 'mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness' (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and 'drop constblind' (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier).",
4554:     "note": "REAL - measured while checking whether R2's rows would catch their own removal.",
4590:     "note": "HARNESS-INTEGRITY CLUSTER TRIAGE: eight rows about the instruments themselves, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE PANEL VOTED 5-0 TO DROP `constblind` AND THE VOTE WAS WRONG -- CHECKED, NOT TAKEN. ***  agy, grok, glm, deepseek and minimax all said drop it as 'already audited and fixed'; minimax even named the closing commit (cdc4849). Every one of them was reading the SAME SENTENCE -- the row's own hold, 'AUDITED: diff_timer was the only one exposed; now fixed'. That audit covered SEVEN differentials. THERE ARE THIRTEEN NOW. Six arrived after it, and re-running the audit's own test against today's tree found a LIVE INSTANCE: diff_rtc_range.c, written HOURS EARLIER IN THIS SESSION BY ME, reads TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY on the expected side of three rows.\n\nMEASURED, and it is the constblind shape in one screen: changing TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY to 1073741823.0 left all three clamp rows GREEN -- they printed the new value and passed -- while only an absolute-consequence row noticed. Fixed in the same commit by asserting `TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY == (double) INT_MAX`, derived from OUTSIDE the header, exactly as diff_timer.c:276-280 already does. Gate 2 PASS at 259 checks.\n\n*** THE LESSON IS BIGGER THAN THE ROW: A UNANIMOUS VERDICT DERIVED FROM ONE STALE RECORD IS ONE STALE RECORD, NOT FIVE CONFIRMATIONS. ***  Five seats agreeing looks like strong evidence and was worth exactly as much as the sentence they all read. The packet-fed seats COULD NOT have checked it; the two file-reading seats COULD have and did not. This is the clearest case yet for the standing rule that anything checkable in under two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged -- and the check here was one grep.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- four-way split, recorded not voted: gateflr (agy, minimax), pcheck (grok), optrow (glm, deepseek: 'the only measured false pass with a known one-flag fix').\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 10.3 KB, grok 10.0 KB, kimi 25.4 KB, glm 13.5 KB, deepseek 7.7 KB, minimax). Codex RATE-LIMITED, seventh panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: LEAD CANDIDATE from two seats (agy, minimax), on harness-integrity grounds: if a whole gate block is deleted, nothing outside it notices. That is the 'a green row means nothing' class applied one level up -- to the gate rather than the row.",
4617:     "note": "WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.\n\nITS VERDICT: VERDICT: Do `gateflr` first; DROP `census`, `constblind` as an open finding, and `strad` as currently filed.\n\nCAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel's own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a '+1' mutant that 'admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green' -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: 'do tfreq first' (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 'CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store'), 'mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness' (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and 'drop constblind' (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier).",
4634:     "ev": "Agent(model=opus) held-stage seat, batch A (harness rows). Read-only; GXEMUL-SEC only.",
4693:     "note": "HARNESS-INTEGRITY CLUSTER TRIAGE: eight rows about the instruments themselves, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE PANEL VOTED 5-0 TO DROP `constblind` AND THE VOTE WAS WRONG -- CHECKED, NOT TAKEN. ***  agy, grok, glm, deepseek and minimax all said drop it as 'already audited and fixed'; minimax even named the closing commit (cdc4849). Every one of them was reading the SAME SENTENCE -- the row's own hold, 'AUDITED: diff_timer was the only one exposed; now fixed'. That audit covered SEVEN differentials. THERE ARE THIRTEEN NOW. Six arrived after it, and re-running the audit's own test against today's tree found a LIVE INSTANCE: diff_rtc_range.c, written HOURS EARLIER IN THIS SESSION BY ME, reads TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY on the expected side of three rows.\n\nMEASURED, and it is the constblind shape in one screen: changing TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY to 1073741823.0 left all three clamp rows GREEN -- they printed the new value and passed -- while only an absolute-consequence row noticed. Fixed in the same commit by asserting `TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY == (double) INT_MAX`, derived from OUTSIDE the header, exactly as diff_timer.c:276-280 already does. Gate 2 PASS at 259 checks.\n\n*** THE LESSON IS BIGGER THAN THE ROW: A UNANIMOUS VERDICT DERIVED FROM ONE STALE RECORD IS ONE STALE RECORD, NOT FIVE CONFIRMATIONS. ***  Five seats agreeing looks like strong evidence and was worth exactly as much as the sentence they all read. The packet-fed seats COULD NOT have checked it; the two file-reading seats COULD have and did not. This is the clearest case yet for the standing rule that anything checkable in under two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged -- and the check here was one grep.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- four-way split, recorded not voted: gateflr (agy, minimax), pcheck (grok), optrow (glm, deepseek: 'the only measured false pass with a known one-flag fix').\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 10.3 KB, grok 10.0 KB, kimi 25.4 KB, glm 13.5 KB, deepseek 7.7 KB, minimax). Codex RATE-LIMITED, seventh panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEPT. No seat prioritised it and none argued to drop it: nothing goes red if the driver and the log grader disagree about the same run.",
4720:     "note": "WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.\n\nITS VERDICT: VERDICT: Do `gateflr` first; DROP `census`, `constblind` as an open finding, and `strad` as currently filed.\n\nCAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel's own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a '+1' mutant that 'admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green' -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: 'do tfreq first' (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 'CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store'), 'mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness' (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and 'drop constblind' (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier).",
