{
    "id":  "pcc2c040kill",
    "title":  "pcc2c040kill  dev_pcc2.c: a LEGAL byte write to GENCTL with the C040 bit set ends the host in reassert_interrupts() -- the sibling kill the width fix exposed rather than removed",
    "state":  "open",
    "state_note":  "Surfaced by the pcc2widthkills pass-1 panel (fable5 and kimi independently) while auditing the width fix: \u0027ten kills removed\u0027 would read as the file being safe while a one-byte guest write still exits.  The width detector\u0027s GENCTL lanes were chosen (0xd9/0x40, C040 clear) precisely to stay off this kill; c040ok() asserts it at authoring time.  Next round after pcc2widthkills closes.  Filed in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (dated tail, `### pcc2c040kill`, line 14553 as of 2026-09-04); this row added 2026-09-04 so the dashboard shows it -- the bug file had it, the ledger did not.  NEW FACT 2026-09-05 (Opus design seat on pcc2defaultkill, verified: dev_pcc2.c SCCRXIACK arm has no writeflag test): a guest LOAD of SCCRXIACK 0x27 runs reassert_interrupts(), and OpenBSD\u0027s console driver cl.c reads that offset on SCC receive interrupts -- so the ENTRY into the C040 kill is routine guest traffic; only the ARMING (C040 set in GENCTL) is rare.  Reachability better than the filing says.  Must be fixed before any round implements a PCC2 IACK arm.\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).\nTHE CENTRAL PREMISE WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION MAKES THE DEFECT SIMPLER AND WORSE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE for unsupported C040 delivery.  Survival is a nonvacuous\noracle here, but cannot establish interrupt correctness.\"*\n\n*** THE CORRECTION: *\"the GENCTL write itself immediately calls the terminating helper.  Arming and\ntermination occur within that access; subsequent SCC traffic is unnecessary.\"* ***\n\n**Line 459 IS `reassert_interrupts()` inside the GENCTL byte-write arm.**  So the whole\n`SCCRXIACK`-plus-OpenBSD-`cl.c` entry story -- which this row records as a NEW FACT improving\nreachability, and which the brief built its S4 around -- is UNNECESSARY.  **A single legal byte write\nof 0x04 to offset 0x02 terminates the emulator on the spot.**  No second access, no driver, no\ninterrupt.\n\n*That does not make the earlier finding wrong -- SCCRXIACK really does run the helper with no\ndirection test, and that really is routine traffic.  It makes it SUPERFLUOUS: the row had found a\nlong road to a place reachable in one step, and nobody noticed the short one because the long one was\ninteresting.*\n\n**CONFIRMED BY READING, beyond what the brief asserted:**\n  * `pcctwo_reg[]` holds **`uint8_t`**; GENCTL rejects non-byte writes, but the accepted byte write\n    **stores `idata` WITHOUT MASKING** and immediately calls the helper.  **No read-only treatment\n    prevents the bit being stored**, and the header explicitly permits byte accesses.\n  * The ten call sites are confirmed with their contexts -- 383 `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`, 400 the\n    device TICK (unconditionally), 459 GENCTL write, 551 T1ICR/T2ICR, 574 SCCICR, 588 SCCTX,\n    602 SCCRX, 609 SCCRXIACK (no direction test), 622 SCSIICR, 662 MASK -- all reaching **one shared\n    terminating branch**.\n  * **THE CONDITION IS NOT STICKY:** a GENCTL write CLEARING C040 stores the cleared value before\n    calling the helper.  So the state is recoverable in principle -- and unrecoverable in practice,\n    because the arming write has already terminated the process.\n  * **`400` is the device TICK, called unconditionally.**  So even a guest that arms C040 and then\n    does nothing at all is terminated at the next tick.  That is worth stating separately: it removes\n    the last version of the story in which the guest must go on to do something.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED, and it is the opposite of the neighbouring rounds\u0027 problem:** *\"Survival is a\nnonvacuous oracle here.\"*  This project has measured that survival rows at REFUSAL-EFFECT sites are\nvacuous by construction -- guarded and unguarded code both survive.  Here the pre-fix build genuinely\ndoes NOT survive, so survival discriminates.  **But it cannot establish interrupt correctness**, which\nis the second half of the disposition question and needs its own row.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS NOW TRIVIALLY CHEAP.**  This row\u0027s `witness` field says a rung-3 attempt *\"looks\ncheap (one more session in `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py`, which already byte-writes this register on\nthis machine)\"* -- and with arming-equals-termination it is cheaper still: **one byte write and\nobserve that the process is gone.**  The field\u0027s promise that it says `not yet done` rather than\n`did not think to ask` is now cashable in a single session.\n\n*And a note on the brief: it was written with C5 applied FROM THE START -- neutral register, scoped\nreading, no chain -- on a subject (process termination) that is trigger 1\u0027s own vocabulary.  **It was\nnot refused.**  First time today I wrote one that way instead of learning it from a refusal.*\n\n**READY TO CLAIM AS A ROUND, 2026-09-07.**  The stated blocker was *\"next round after\npcc2widthkills closes\"*; that row now carries `commits=b78bb0f`, so the width fix is IN THE TREE and\nonly its formal closure is outstanding.  Nothing about this defect depends on that closure.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS ONE SESSION IN AN EXISTING PROBE.**  `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py` already drives\nGENCTL byte writes on this machine and carries an authoring-time assertion, `c040ok()`, whose whole\njob is to keep its lanes (`0xd9`/`0x40`) OFF this path.  **So the probe already proves the access\nworks and already knows the bit is dangerous** -- the witness is the same access with the bit set,\nand the oracle is that the process is gone.\n\n  *That assertion is worth keeping rather than deleting when the row is added: it protects the WIDTH\n  rows from wandering onto this path, which is a different job from the new row deliberately\n  stepping onto it.  A row that turns the guard off for the whole file would re-open exactly what\n  `c040ok()` was written to prevent.*\n\n**FIX SHAPE, from the audit:** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, with its own latch bit -- the same\nper-instance reasoning as `dev_sgi_re`\u0027s bits, since a shared latch is silenced by the other site\u0027s\nordinary traffic.  **The open design question is Q2, which the audit did not settle from the source:**\nafter declining, does the helper fall through to the normal level recalculation, or return having\nasserted nothing?  Those differ in what the guest observes, and the answer decides whether the round\nowes a second row on interrupt behaviour -- which the verdict\u0027s own second clause anticipates\n(*\"survival ... cannot establish interrupt correctness\"*).\n\n*** WITNESS DESIGN REVIEWED AND NOT RATIFIED.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/grok_witness_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"NOT RATIFY as proposed.  A guest `st.b` of 0x04 at GENCTL is a sound CORE of a rung-3\nwitness of this kill.  Survival-only, in `pcc2_width_probe.py`, with no same-decode preset and no\nsite-attribution, is not.  **That is the same class as the design you asked me to assume: the\nobservation is either destroyed by the defect, or it reports success while measuring nothing.**\"*\n\n**THE CORE SURVIVES: one legal byte write is the right reproduction.**  What fails is my ORACLE, and\nthe required attribution is specific:\n\n  1. **`how == \"exit1\"`, not merely `not alive`.**  `not alive` is satisfied by several unrelated\n     deaths.\n  2. **The transcript must contain `C040 interrupt assertions`.**  Missing text plus exit1 is\n     *FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON*, not a pass -- which is the carrier\u0027s own rule 4 (*every\n     reproduction must answer \"did it fail for the reason under test?\"*), applied to a row I had\n     written without it.\n  3. **Controls in sessions that MUST SURVIVE**, so a construction failure cannot masquerade as the\n     defect: a RAM round-trip; CHIPID `ld.bu` -\u003e 0x20 (RAM would not answer that); and a\n     **C040-CLEAR GENCTL round-trip of a nonzero value** -- 0x82, already proven by existing rows in\n     `pcc2_width_probe.py` and `pcc2defaultkill`.  *That third one is the same-decode preset: it\n     proves the register is reachable and writable BEFORE the killing value is used.*\n  4. *** THEY MUST ALL RUN BEFORE THE KILLING STORE.  Post-kill they cannot run. ***\n\n**POINT 4 IS THE ONE I WOULD NOT HAVE FOUND, and it is the Alpha design\u0027s flaw wearing different\nclothes.**  There the defect destroyed the TRANSLATION the observation needed; here it destroys the\nPROCESS the controls need.  In both cases the observation and the thing observed cannot coexist, and\nin both cases I designed the observation first and never asked what state it required afterwards.\n\n*** THREE WITNESS DESIGNS OF MINE HAVE NOW BEEN REVIEWED AND ALL THREE HAD A FLAW OF THE SAME CLASS.\n***  Alpha swpctx (defect destroys the translation), `#505`\u0027s tiled row (two silent declines read as\nplausible values), and this one (survival is not attribution).  **The common shape is that I design\nwhat to OBSERVE and not how to ATTRIBUTE it** -- the ladder\u0027s clause (ii) asks for a liveness control\nAND a signature control, and in each case I supplied the first and reasoned my way past the second.\nRecorded here rather than as a carrier rule because three instances is a pattern about ME, and the\ncheap remedy is already available: **route every witness design to a reviewer before building it.**\nThat has now cost one invocation each time and caught three real defects.\n\n**ALSO FLAGGED, and it needs checking before the round:** the seat calls S3\u0027s tick claim *possibly\nsequencing-wrong* -- i.e. whether call site 400 (the unconditional device tick) really does reach the\nkill for a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing.  I asserted that from the call-site list; it\nis not verified.\n\n*** THE TICK CLAIM IS VACUOUS, AND SO IS \u0027TEN CALL SITES\u0027 AS A STATEMENT OF HAZARD.  My record,\ncorrected by checking the flag grok raised. ***\n\ngrok called S3\u0027s tick claim *\"possibly sequencing-wrong\"*.  Checked, and it is wrong:\n\n  * `DEVICE_TICK(pcc2)` really does call `reassert_interrupts(d)` unconditionally -- that part of my\n    claim is literally true;\n  * **but GENCTL is written in exactly ONE place** -- its own case arm -- and that arm calls\n    `reassert_interrupts()` in the SAME ACCESS.  Every other reference to `pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL]`\n    in the file is a READ (`:271` the C040 test, `:341` the MIEN test).  A wide write is DECLINED\n    BEFORE the store.\n\n**So the process always terminates on the ARMING WRITE ITSELF, and the tick can never be the first\nhit.**  *\"Even a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing is terminated at the next tick\"* describes\na state that cannot be reached: there is no interval during which C040 is set and the process is\nalive.\n\n*** AND THE SHARPER CORRECTION: \u0027TEN CALL SITES\u0027 IS TRUE AND MISLEADING. ***  All ten reach one\nshared branch, which is what the audit established and which stands.  But **nine of them are\nunreachable AS A FIRST HIT**, because the only way the guard\u0027s condition becomes true is a write that\nimmediately calls the helper itself.  **The defect has exactly ONE reachable entry.**\n\n**WHAT THIS CHANGES:**\n  * the WITNESS is simpler than the design assumed -- one session, one store; **no tick row is owed**,\n    and a row waiting for a tick would wait forever;\n  * the SEVERITY framing is unchanged (one legal byte still ends the emulator) but the *breadth*\n    framing was inflated: ten sites reads as ten ways in, and: \"A round about PCC2/CD2401/MVME187 opens THIS before reasoning from the\nsource alone.\"  I did not.**  Opened now, at its General Control Register page:\n\n    ADR/SIZ   $FFF42002 (8 bits)\n    DIR       15    14  13  12  11    10    9     8\n    NAME      DR0                     C040  MIEN  FAST\n    OPER      R/W   R   R   R   R     R/W   R/W   R/W\n    RESET     0     0   0   0   0     0     0     0\n\nThe manual numbers the bits 15..8 as the upper byte of a word view, so 15-\u003e7, 10-\u003e2, 9-\u003e1, 8-\u003e0:\n\n    DR0  = 0x80     C040 = 0x04     MIEN = 0x02     FAST = 0x01\n\n**ALL FOUR MATCH THE FORK\u0027S HEADER EXACTLY.  P1 IS CONFIRMED and the OpenBSD `include/pcctworeg.h`\n`C040 0x03` is simply a buggy header** -- 0x03 is `FAST|MIEN`, which is not a coherent C040 mask.\n*The extracted-table stagger hazard is covered here by FOUR calibration points plus the address\n`$FFF42002` matching offset 0x02 and the all-zero RESET row matching the modelled reset.*\n\n*** AND THE SAME PAGE SETTLES Q1, WHICH NO SEAT COULD ANSWER FROM THE PACKET: ***\n\n\u003e *\"If the C040 bit is set, the interrupt request goes to the MPU via the EIPL\\* pins **at the level\n\u003e that is programmed for SCSI interrupts in the SCSI Interrupt Control Register**.  If the C040 bit\n\u003e is cleared, the interrupt goes to the MPU via the INT pin...\"*\n\n**C040 selects the DELIVERY PATH, not the level.**  The programmed interrupt level is used either\nway, so the IPL recalculation stays meaningful in C040 mode and only the ASSERTION is suppressed --\nwhich is exactly the proposed shape, now on a primary-source warrant instead of an inference from\nthe sibling idiom.  **MIEN\u0027s own text confirms the relationship**: *\"When it is low, all interrupts\nfrom the PCCchip2 are disabled (this includes both the EIPL\\* pins and the INT pin)\"* -- MIEN gates\nBOTH paths, C040 selects BETWEEN them.  So joining the two tests is right and they are not\nredundant.\n\n*Q1 was the question three seats said the packet could not settle.  It cost one grep of a file the\ncarrier had already told me to open.*\n\n### 3.  TWO PANEL CONCERNS CHECKED AND REFUTED (not judged)\n\n* **minimax #4 / deepseek\u0027s bit-collision mutant.**  `PCC2_W_*` is a **C99 enum with sequential\n  members terminated by `PCC2_W_NBITS`**, plus a `pcc2_w_fits_in_warned` width assert.  **A\n  correctly-added enumerator CANNOT collide** -- that is the same \"duplicates impossible by\n  construction\" idiom this project adopted deliberately elsewhere.  *The row is still worth having\n  against a hand-written literal, which is what deepseek\u0027s step 1 actually tests.*\n* **minimax #3, \"the witness goes stale/red after the fix\".**  Mechanically FALSE:\n  `row(\"c040 attrib\", \"yes\" if (alive2 or sig) else \"died-elsewhere\", \"yes\", ...)` -- post-fix\n  `alive2` is true, so it evaluates \"yes\" against a wanted \"yes\" and stays **GREEN**.  *The real\n  point survives in weaker form: the row becomes VACUOUS post-fix, since it can no longer fail, and\n  the EVIDENCE print falls silent.*\n\n### 4.  THE ROWS THE ROUND NOW OWES (from the panel, not from me)\n\n* **deepseek\u0027s four-step latch row** -- pre-dirty another `warned` bit via ordinary guest traffic;\n  write C040; assert host survives AND the announce appears EXACTLY ONCE; write C040 again; assert\n  no second announce.  *Separates silent decline, non-latching and bit collision in one session;\n  \"presence alone is not enough.\"*\n* **minimax\u0027s non-suppressing mutant row** -- C040 set with MIEN=1 and IPL above the mask, asserting\n  the CPU interrupt line is NOT asserted.  **Without it, a fix that announces loudly and forgets\n  `assert = 0` is green.**\n\n### 5.  PROVENANCE FOUND IN THE FILE\u0027S OWN COMMENT\n\n`pcc2widthkills` already documented this defect in product code while deliberately scoping it out:\n*\"NOT FIXED HERE, cited so \u0027twelve kills removed\u0027 is not read as \u0027file clean\u0027: pcc2c040kill (a LEGAL\nbyte write of 0x04 to GENCTL still ends the host in reassert_interrupts).\"*  **An independent\nconfirmation of the defect, written by the round that chose not to fix it** -- and the sentence this\nround discharges.\n\n*** FIXED AS `#512`.  Kill table 2026-09-08 in a scratch clone: four mutants KILLED, ONE ESCAPE\nWHICH THE ROUND GRADES AS A FLOOR BREACH RATHER THAN DISCLOSING IT AWAY. ***\n\n    BASE 7/7 | M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n    M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** CLEAN SWEEP -- the non-suppressing mutant escapes ***\n\n`assert = 0` is the repair\u0027s EFFECT, so nulling it is inside clause (d)\u0027s DEPENDS-ON closure.  The\nescape is structural, verified twice: `PCC2_IRQ_INT` (0x20) is masked out of every guest-writable\narm (`TxICR \u0026 0x17`, `SCCICR \u0026 0x1f`) and its only producer is `pcc_timer_tick`, a `timer_add`\nHOST-TIMER callback that the debugger stops for the duration of a `step`.  **The repair is a\nfree-running probe shape, filed as `pcc2c040assertrow`.**\n\n*** THE codex PASS-1 ANSWER, MINED 2026-09-08 -- I HAD RECORDED IT AS \"findings not yet fully mined\",\nWHICH IS AN UNREAD REVIEW WITH A HONEST LABEL ON IT.  It corrects TWO of the round\u0027s premises and\nsupplies a MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD. ***\n\n**P2 WAS OVERSTATED AND THE CORRECT WORDING IS WEAKER.**  I wrote *\"No file in the OpenBSD 2.2 tree\nwrites C040 to GENCTL.\"*  codex: **\"The defensible wording is \u0027no explicit C040-setting use found,\u0027\nnot \u0027no file writes C040\u0027\"** -- because a grep over named macros cannot exclude literal or indirect\nstores, and, decisively, **P3\u0027s read-modify-write (`genctl |= IEN`) WRITES BACK AN ALREADY-SET C040\nBIT.**  Its census also beats mine: C040 defines sit in THREE source headers (mvme88k `dev/`,\nmvme88k `include/`, mvme68k `dev/`) plus an on-disk `machine/` copy.\n\n**P5 WAS TOO NARROW.**  I wrote that C040 means *\"the CPU is an MC68040\"*.  codex: it selects the\n**M68000-family interrupt INTERFACE**, and *\"the manual explicitly says to clear it for an\nMC88100\"*.  The robustness rationale is unaffected; the shipped-guest compatibility claim remains\nunestablished either way.\n\n*** AND A CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD, DISTINCT FROM M_d1: \"SUPPRESSION INSIDE\nTHE LATCH\". ***  Keep the guard AND the announcement, but move `assert = 0` INSIDE the\n`if (!warned)` block:\n\n\u003e *\"The first encounter suppresses correctly; later encounters can assert the CPU line.  The\n\u003e announcement count remains exactly one.  This directly meets clause (d)\u0027s \u0027guard kept, repair\n\u003e nulled\u0027 shape.\"*\n\n**Every shipped row stays green under it** -- `c040 says` yes, `c040 once` 1, `c040 predirty` yes.\nSo the detector has a SECOND declared escape, and it is nastier than `M_d1` because the fix looks\ncorrect on first contact and fails only on repeat encounters.\n\n\u003e **THE SHIPPED FIX IS NOT VULNERABLE TO IT: `assert = 0;` is OUTSIDE the latch block**, which is\n\u003e what codex\u0027s implementation note asked for (*\"Keep `assert = 0` outside the warning-latch\n\u003e condition\"*).  Verified in the diff.  **The gap is in the DETECTOR, not in the code** -- and it\n\u003e folds into `pcc2c040assertrow`, which needs the same free-running shape.\n\ncodex also independently re-derived the `(once)` census the carrier says never to quote: **30\n`(once)` after excluding comments and joining adjacent C string literals**, versus 12\n`(once per offset)`; the 34 raw occurrences include four comments.\n\n*It flagged one provenance gap in my own artifact, correctly: the witness raw names `build/gxemul`\nwith no commit or binary hash, so it does not independently establish which build it measured.*\n\n*** PASS 2 REFUSED THE ROUND\u0027S CLOSING DISPOSITION, AND IT IS RIGHT.  STATE REVERTED `fixed` -\u003e\n`open` 2026-09-08. ***\n\n**grok\u0027s ruling:** *\"disposition 2 is wrong.  1 and 2 cannot both stand.  1 is the right grade ...\n2 is then the (e) REMEDY applied to a (d) GRADE.  That is the contradiction.  L2 says replace the\ndetector in-round.  Coverage-debt filing is the above-floor path.  **Honesty about the breach does\nnot create an exception L2 does not have; a declared L2 that ships is the precedent this question\nexists to refuse.**\"*\n\nAnd the sharpest sentence, which I should have seen myself: *\"The CHANGELOG already says the round\ndoes not claim a floor-meeting detector -- good -- and the ledger row is already `state: fixed`.\n**Those two sentences cannot sit together.**\"*  **They did, for about an hour, in records I wrote.**\n\n*** AND MY WARRANT FOR SHIPPING WAS FALSE.  VERIFIED HERE, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nI claimed the non-suppressing mutant could not be rowed because `PCC2_IRQ_INT` has *\"only one\nproducer, `pcc_timer_tick`, a host-timer callback that `step` stops\"*.  **The first half is right and\nthe second is wrong:**\n\n    dev_pcc2.c  pcctwo_interrupt_common()  ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT onto SCSIICR / SCCTX / SCCRX\n                                           when those lines assert\n    dev_clmpcc.c:88-89   if ((d-\u003ereg[CLMPCC_REG_IER] \u0026 3) != 0)\n                                 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d-\u003eirq_scc_tx);\n    machine_mvme88k.c    constructs clmpcc with irq=...pcc2...\n\n**So a GUEST STORE to the clmpcc IER asserts a line whose callback sets the INT bit, synchronously,\ninside that store.**  No host timer, no free-running probe: `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to\nCLMPCC IER, `st.b` `C040|MIEN` to GENCTL.  **The obstacle was never the STATE -- it is the ORACLE.**\n\n\u003e **THE ERROR IN MY REASONING, stated plainly because it is a repeatable one: I checked that no\n\u003e guest store writes INT INTO THE ICR, and concluded that no guest store can set it.**  A store to a\n\u003e DIFFERENT DEVICE sets it through the interrupt callback.  *\"Which stores write this field\" is not\n\u003e the same question as \"which stores cause this field to change\".*\n\n*** grok\u0027s Q2: THE SMALLEST UNDECLARED EDIT THAT STILL SCORES 7/7 IS ONE TOKEN. ***\n\n**`assert = 0;` -\u003e `return;`**  Announce, latch, survival, `SAYS`, once-count and predirty are all\nunchanged; **the CPU INT line is simply never deasserted.**  No shipped row sees it, and grok gives\nthe reason: *\"every session writes 0x04 into a reset GENCTL, so MIEN is already clear, `assert` is\nalready 0, and `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` of a quiet line is a no-op.  The discriminating sequence is\npending INT + MIEN, THEN set C040.  The 7 rows never build that.\"*\n\nFour more same-score edits it named: move the block to the top and `return` (skips IPL recalc,\n`cur_int_vec[]` AND deassert); drop `(once)` from the message (`c040 once` counts `SAYS`, not the\nmarker); `fatal` -\u003e `debug` (the probe launches with `-V`, so it still prints); swap the C040 and\nMIEN tests (independent, order invisible).\n\n*** AN L4 IN THE SHIPPED PRODUCT COMMENT, FOUND BY READING THE CONTROL FLOW. ***  My comment said an\nearly return here *\"would skip the IPL recalculation, the cur_int_vec[] updates and the\nINTERRUPT_DEASSERT below\"*.  **At the NEW site the first two have already run** -- a return there\nskips only the DEASSERT.  That sentence is true of a return at the OLD site and false at this one:\n*\"a wrong reason attached to a right placement.  Wrong reasons get reused -- the next mutant is\nexactly `return` at this site.\"*  **Corrected in place.**\n\n**Dispositions 3, 4 and 5 were CHECKED and HOLD** -- including that the width probe\u0027s `latch all`\nrow still wants 12 announces from 12 wide writes and does not iterate `NBITS`, so appending the enum\nmember renumbers nothing; and that `c040 attrib` is green post-fix only via `alive2`, which grok\ncalls *\"the price of clause (i)\"* and agrees is right.\n\n**DISPOSITION: the three product hunks are correct and stay.  The ROUND does not close.**  The L2\nrepair is now known to be buildable in a stepped probe, so it is an in-round obligation rather than\na filed one.\n\n*** THE FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED IN-ROUND, WHICH IS WHAT PASS 2 REQUIRED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0\u0027 DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0\u0027 -\u003e `return;\u0027   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0\u0027 INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\nThe round no longer ships a declared clause-(d) escape.  `pcc2c040assertrow` -- filed when the\nrepair was believed to need a probe shape this project did not have -- is CLOSED, and the detector\nis now 11 rows.\n\n*Pass 2\u0027s ruling was that grading (d) while FILING the repair is \"the (e) remedy applied to a (d)\ngrade\".  The correct response was to build the rows, and building them took one design review and\ntwo measured tables.*\n\n*** PASS 2 ON THE L2 REPAIR: SIX SEATS, TWO DO-NOT-SHIP, AND **FOUR OF SIX** REFUSED THE SAME\nDISPOSITION.  The detector went 11 -\u003e 16 rows and now kills NINE mutants across FIVE tables. ***\n\n**FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUSED DISPOSITION 1** (grok, deepseek, glm, the kimi substitute);\nminimax defended it *conditionally* and named its own condition.  **I had declared the encounter\naxis closed while enumerating only the C040=1 slice of a two-lvalue product** -- and had filed the\nempty cell as contract debt, which is the SECOND time this round graded a floor-shaped hole that\nway.\n\n    THE PRODUCT              warned = 0          warned = 1\n      C040 clear             `irq live\u0027          `c040 recover\u0027   \u003c- was EMPTY\n      C040 set               `c040 noassert\u0027     `c040 noassert2\u0027\n\n**THE FULL KILL RECORD, five tables:**\n\n    table 1   7 rows   M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n                       M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 2  10 rows   M_d1_noassert 9/10 KILLED | M_return 9/10 KILLED\n                       M_latchinside 10/10  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 3  11 rows   M_latchinside 10/11 KILLED\n    table 4  12 rows   M_sticky 11/12 KILLED        (grok\u0027s `|| (warned \u0026 ...)\u0027)\n    table 5  13 rows   M_mienclobber 12/13 KILLED   (`GENCTL \u0026= ~PCC2_MIEN\u0027)\n\n**`M_mienclobber` is the clearest demonstration of why the collateral row was owed:** `irq live` and\n`c040 noassert` BOTH stayed GREEN under it -- every delivery row passes a fix that suppresses by\ndisabling all interrupts -- and only the GENCTL readback saw it, coming back `0x04` instead of\n`0x06`.  **Three seats named that mutant independently.**\n\n*** THE LAST ROWS CAME FROM THE SUBSTITUTE SEAT AND NO OTHER SEAT SAW IT. ***  The liveness control\nexisted for ONE session of FOUR: `c040 noassert` reaching COMPLETION was consistent with \"the fix\nsuppressed correctly\" AND with \"no interrupt was ever pending in that session\".  Three per-session\nSCCTX readbacks close it, at zero extra cost because the sessions already ran.  **16/16.**\n\n*Two apparatus facts recorded for the next reader:* the PC regex demands eight hex digits, so it\nreads the register DUMP and not the unpadded `BREAKPOINT:` line (the values are right; the line is\nnot the one I thought); and `(\"continue\", 6.0)` carries no `until`, so the full grace always\nelapses.  *And glm reconstructed the mixed-denominator defect from the printed numbers alone --\nwhich I had already found and corrected in the commit message but NOT in the brief the panel read.*\n\n*** THE FIRST PASS-2 PANEL HAD FIVE UNRECORDED SEATS, AND THE PRECOMMIT GATE CAUGHT IT -- WHICH IS\nTHIS SESSION\u0027S OWN MOST-REPEATED FINDING, COMMITTED BY ME, ON THE ROUND WHERE I KEPT FIXING IT. ***\n\n`SEATS_READ_FAIL 5 seat answers exist on disk and are recorded NOWHERE` -- codex (1.0 MB), deepseek,\nglm, kimi and minimax from `panel_20260908_000754`.  **I recorded grok\u0027s answer from that panel and\nnone of the other five**, then spent the following hours mining OTHER rounds\u0027 unread reviews.\n\n**Recorded now, and the tally matters: FIVE OF SIX SEATS PUT THEIR RESIDUAL ON DISPOSITION 2** (ship\nwith the breach declared).  Only kimi declined to call it wrong, and for an honest packet-fed reason\n-- clause (d)\u0027s text was not inlined, so it named the single fact that would settle it instead of\nguessing.  **deepseek prescribed the exact repair that was then built:** *\"add a row that can set the\nICR INT bit, kill M_d1, and THEN ship.\"*\n\n*** AND panel_ollama.py AUTO-RECOVERED A THINKING BURN, WHICH THE CARRIER DOES NOT RECORD IT DOING.\n***  glm\u0027s first shot returned an EMPTY `response` beside **119,022 characters of hidden reasoning**\nwith `done_reason=stop` -- the fifth seat-failure class, in the exact shape the carrier says defeats\nevery size check.  **The tool re-fired with `think=False` and got 124,850 characters back.**  The\ncarrier says detection of this class is solved by `seat_verdicts.py`; it does not say the LAUNCHER\nnow recovers it.  *That is a capability worth knowing about before anyone writes another paragraph\nabout burns being unrecoverable.*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe` (PRECOMMIT_PASS, 0 hard, 60 files).  THE ROW STAYS **OPEN** ON PURPOSE. ***\n\nThe code shipped and is measured; what is outstanding is the FLAGSHIP PASS, which is held and queued\nfor the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  **A row whose fix is committed but whose panel is incomplete is\n`open` with its commit named** -- the same shape `pcc2widthkills` carries -- because closing it would\nassert a completeness the roster does not have.  *That is the provisional-adjudication rule applied\nto state rather than to a ruling.*\n\n**The gate refused this commit THREE TIMES and was right every time** (6 hard -\u003e 3 -\u003e 0): five rows\nfiled only into the ledger; three stages held-but-not-queued; **five seat answers recorded nowhere**;\nand two tooling CONVENTIONS I guessed instead of read -- `hold_question` is a ROW-level field, and\n`check_fable_queue` parses a level-3 heading whose first backticked token is the row id.\n\n*** A codex ANALYSIS JOB WAS FIRED AT THIS ROW ON 2026-09-08 AND THE BRIEF WAS STALE.  THE DEFECT\nWAS MINE, AND IT IS THE ONE CHECK THE BRIEF TEMPLATE PUTS FIRST. ***\n\nI wrote the brief from this row\u0027s TITLE -- *\"a legal byte write ... ends the host in\nreassert_interrupts()\"* -- and did NOT run the template\u0027s `AC0` step (*HAS THIS ROUND ALREADY\nSHIPPED?  TWO COMMANDS, BEFORE YOU WRITE ANYTHING*).  I had run it for `#515` the same evening.\n\ncodex answered correctly and its verdict is the refutation: *\"the termination claim is stale for this\ncheckout ... Exactly one executable `exit(1)` remains, and there is no executable `exit(1)` in this\nhelper.  The opening comment claiming this write \u0027still ends the host\u0027 contradicts the\nimplementation.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED here, and codex is right on every count:**\n\n    active exit( in dev_pcc2.c, comments stripped:  1   (at :414, NOT the C040 arm)\n    dev_pcc2.c:369-376   the C040 arm today is a LATCHED ANNOUNCE + suppression --\n                         `d-\u003ewarned \u0026 (1u \u003c\u003c PCC2_W_C040)\u0027, `fatal(... (once))\u0027, `assert = 0\u0027\n    git log -- dev_pcc2.c   9fa07fe  \"#512 pcc2c040kill: one legal guest byte store ended the host\"\n    CHANGELOG.md:427        the `#512\u0027 round block\n\n**SO THE FIX SHIPPED AS `#512` AND THIS ROW IS OPEN ON PURPOSE** -- its own note says so in capitals,\nawaiting the flagship pass held for 2026-09-09. *The row was right, the code was right, and the brief\nI fed the seat described a state that stopped existing at `9fa07fe`.*\n\n\u003e **THE COST: about 84 KB of a file-reading seat\u0027s effort spent establishing that shipped work had\n\u003e shipped.**  Not wasted -- it produced the [[pcc2c040dup]] header finding in passing -- but the\n\u003e question it was asked was answerable by two commands I already knew to run.\n\n**AND THE ROW\u0027S OWN NOTE IS WHAT STOPPED ME GOING FURTHER WRONG.**  Reading codex\u0027s verdict, my next\nmove was to close this row as overtaken. The note\u0027s *\"THE ROW STAYS OPEN ON PURPOSE\"* is the only\nthing that prevented it. **A state field alone would not have: `open` on shipped work looks exactly\nlike `open` on unstarted work**, which is the state-contradicts-substance class this project already\nrecords -- and here the prose carried the distinction the state could not."
}
{
    "id":  "exitunowned76",
    "title":  "THE UNOWNED REMAINDER: 76 guest-reachable error-exit sites across 24 files own no tracker row -- a TRACKING row so the body of work has a denominator and a pointer, not a re-derivation",
    "state":  "open",
    "state_note":  null
}
{
    "id":  "b120",
    "title":  "b120u  SCSI: an unknown command is reported to the guest as success",
    "state":  "held",
    "state_note":  "*** L4 WRONG RECORD, CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-27. An adversarial opus pass REFUTED THE MECHANISM THIS ROW STATED BELOW. The defect is real; my explanation of it was false, and the true picture is four different wrong outcomes rather than one. ***\n\n**WHAT I GOT WRONG.** I wrote that `scsi_transfer_alloc()`\u0027s `memset(p, 0, ...)` leaves a status BYTE of 0x00, which the guest reads as GOOD. **`status` is `unsigned char *` (`diskimage.h:196-197`)** -- so the memset leaves `status == NULL` and `status_len == 0`. **There is no zeroed status byte anywhere.** Nothing reads a 0x00 out of that struct. The conclusion happened to hold for one controller, for an entirely different reason.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the structural half:** the `default:` arm is the **only one of 25** that never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()` or `diskimage__return_check_condition()` -- verified by grepping every case/helper pair -- and it falls through to `return 1`, which the function\u0027s own header (`:263-266`) documents as the success token (`2` = expects DATA_OUT, `1` = otherwise ok, `0` = error).\n\n*** THE HONEST STATEMENT: ONE OMISSION, FOUR DIFFERENT WRONG OUTCOMES. ***\n  * **`dev_asc.c` -- guest IS told GOOD**, but via an explicit `else fifo_write(0x00)` fallback at `:1284-1292`, which `git log -S` places in the unmodified upstream 0.7.0 import, NOT a fork change. Right conclusion, wrong reason.\n  * **`dev_mb89352.c` -- REFUTES the \u0027told success\u0027 claim outright.** `data_in == NULL` sets `phase = PH_STAT` (`:284`); the guest\u0027s PH_STAT read takes `p = status (NULL), len = 0`, trips the `read longer than buffer?` arm (`:173`) and **sets `cpu-\u003erunning = 0`**. The guest is not told anything -- the emulated CPU is STOPPED. **Filed separately as [[mb89352halt]].**\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:573-582`** -- `while (... \u0026\u0026 i \u003c status_len)` with `status_len == 0` writes **no status byte at all**; the guest\u0027s destination memory keeps its prior contents.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c` (ATAPI)** -- `atapi_st-\u003estatus` is read nowhere in the file; `res == 1` with `data_in == NULL` gives `PHASE_COMPLETED` with `WDCS_ERR` unset: a successful packet command.\n\n\u003e **That is a BETTER case than the row originally made, not a weaker one** -- a single omitted completion step producing four distinct wrong behaviours across four controllers. But it is not the claim as written, and the claim as written was the one being ranked.\n\n**A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THE ORIGINAL RECORD MISSED:** the helper also RETIRES LATCHED SENSE (the `#417` fix, `:187-188`). The `default:` arm skips that too -- so an unimplemented command following a failed one leaves `sense_key/asc/ascq` latched, and a follow-up REQUEST SENSE attributes the OLD failure to the command that just ran. That is exactly the regression `#417` was written to remove, reachable by a different door.\n\n**FIX SHAPE, now pinned by the in-tree idiom at `:319-327`:** call the helper, set `status[0] = 0x02` (CHECK CONDITION), then `return 0`. Note `return 0` is **distinguishable on only two of four controllers** -- osiop stops the SCRIPTS engine, wdc completes with a latched warning, while asc and mb89352 do not test for 0 at all -- so the status buffer, not the return value, is what carries the refusal.\n\n--- the original record follows, kept so the correction is checkable ---\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, GUEST-REACHABLE, and the highest-severity bare-title row worked so far. Established by reading; the fix idiom ALREADY EXISTS in this file. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c:1380-1388`, the dispatcher\u0027s `default:` arm:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n        debug(\"[ UNIMPLEMENTED SCSI command 0x%02x, disk id=%i\", xferp-\u003ecmd[0], id);\n        // exit(1);\n}\ndebug(\" ]\");\nreturn 1;\n```\n\n**Three things go wrong together:**\n1. it logs through `debug()`, which is verbosity-gated and normally invisible;\n2. **it never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()`** -- every implemented arm does (`:1212`, `:1257`, `:1298`, `:1340`, `:1347`, `:1376`);\n3. it falls through to **`return 1`, the same value a SUCCESSFUL command returns.**\n\n**And the status the guest reads is GOOD, which was checked rather than assumed:** `scsi_transfer_alloc()` ends with `memset(p, 0, sizeof(struct scsi_transfer))` (`diskimage_scsicmd.c:89`), and `dev_asc.c:326-330` frees and reallocates per command. Status 0x00 IS GOOD. **So an unsupported opcode is reported to the guest as a completed, successful command.**\n\n*** THE COMMENTED-OUT `exit(1)` IS THE TELL. *** This site was de-fatalised by commenting out the kill, which removed the death without adding an error path -- the incomplete form of the de-fatalisation this fork otherwise does carefully.\n\n*** THE FIX HAS A TRANSFERRED WARRANT ALREADY IN THIS FILE, found by grepping the tree for the existing idiom rather than reasoning from scratch. *** `diskimage__return_check_condition()` is defined at `:208` and already used for exactly this class at `:823-824` -- `0x05, 0x21`, ILLEGAL REQUEST -- and again at `:909`. **The correct refusal for an unknown opcode is CHECK CONDITION with ILLEGAL REQUEST / INVALID COMMAND OPERATION CODE, and the helper to do it sits three hundred lines above the defect.**\n\n**NOT FIXED: needs a build.** The detector must show the pre-fix build reporting GOOD for an unsupported opcode and CHECK CONDITION after -- and floor clause (b) needs the `return 1` reinstated verbatim. Both are measurements. Queued behind the embargo.\n\n"
}

